window.FIELD_GUIDE_DATA = {"edition":"October 29, 2026","baseline":"v0.19.1.0","pages":31,"sourceFile":"assets/Phasmophobia_Master_Field_Guide.pdf","ghosts":[{"name":"Aswang","number":1,"page":5,"evidence":["DOTS","Freezing","Writing"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"ahs-WAHNG; Tagalog-based, with ng as in sing. Cebuano often stresses the first syllable.","origin":"Philippines; well-attested regional traditions, with several different creature types.","lore":"Aswang is an umbrella name for beings described as shapeshifters, witches, corpse eaters, vampires or viscera eaters. Some appear human by day and prey on people at night. The versions differ by language and locality; the manananggal is only one related form. A single universal aswang biography would flatten that diversity. [F1, P1]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Lower base speed, but stronger line-of-sight acceleration than a standard ghost: roughly 0.075× base speed per second instead of 0.05×, reaching max speed in about 8.7 s. After its official hiding-spot weakness ends a hunt, it can move toward the remembered hide during the next grace period; this is not a generic zero-grace rule.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"If an Aswang reaches a correctly hidden player in an available official hiding spot, the hunt ends instead of killing that player. It places a waypoint there at the start of the next hunt and can move toward it during grace. Change hides afterward. Community-documented behavior; see section 18.","Best controlled test":"Use an official closet/locker/furniture hiding zone that is actually enabled by the hiding-place setting. Unofficial “safe spots” do not count. During an exposed test, listen for a slightly slow opening pace that ramps unusually quickly while it maintains sight.","0-evidence identification":"Official hiding-spot survival + unusually rapid LOS acceleration are the key 0-evidence tells. Hiding-place setting = None disables the special weakness.","Common confusion":"Slow Twin / calm Obambo can resemble its base pace; a standard ghost can look fast after enough LOS. Test multiple hunts and watch whether the base speed itself changes (Aswang does not).","Survival note":"Break sight early. If you have a known official hiding spot, use it correctly; if it ends the hunt, change your plan before the next one."}},{"name":"Banshee","number":2,"page":5,"evidence":["DOTS","Orbs","UV"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"BAN-shee; usual English pronunciation.","origin":"Ireland; well-attested death-messenger tradition; from Irish bean sí, a woman of the fairy mound.","lore":"Her crying or keening foretells or accompanies a death, often within a particular family. She is commonly a lamenting messenger rather than the killer herself. Descriptions of her age and appearance vary. The game’s pursuit of one selected victim is a gameplay adaptation of the association with individuals and families. [F2, F3]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Chooses a hunt target in multiplayer. While that target is inside the investigation area, the Banshee uses the target’s sanity for hunt checks and ignores other players as kill targets. It has an increased preference for singing ghost events.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Can perform a distinctive Banshee scream detected with the Parabolic Microphone / Sound Recorder. A singing-event collision drains more sanity from the target than a normal event.","Best controlled test":"In multiplayer, compare who is consistently followed or threatened. Scan paranormal sounds repeatedly for the scream. If a hunt passes through non-target teammates while the target is inside, that is extremely strong Banshee behavior.","0-evidence identification":"Unique scream is the cleanest behavior confirmation. Target logic can identify it, but do not manufacture dangerous tests unless the team understands the mechanic.","Common confusion":"Phantom also roams toward players; random pathing can fake “following.” The scream is far stronger than personality.","Survival note":"If you believe you are the target, play conservatively and carry protection. Non-targets can perform controlled hunt observations while the target remains safely positioned."}},{"name":"Dayan","number":3,"page":6,"evidence":["EMF 5","Orbs","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"DAA-yun; Hindi-based, two syllables, not DAY-an.","origin":"South Asia, especially Hindi-speaking traditions; established witch term with regional variations.","lore":"A dayan or daayan is a female witch or dangerous supernatural woman in stories, often associated with harmful magic. Some narratives describe predation, disguise or an outwardly ordinary woman. These are beliefs and storytelling motifs, not a factual category of women. The word’s history should not be reduced to a single modern film portrayal. [F4, P2]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Female-only ghost. Its behavior depends on the closest player within 10 m, including a player on another floor. If that player is still, hunt threshold is 45% and hunt speed is fixed at 1.2 m/s. If that player is moving, threshold is 65% and speed is fixed at 2.25 m/s. With nobody within 10 m it behaves at standard 50% threshold and standard speed rules.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"LOS acceleration can accumulate “in the background” while the Dayan is near a player and using its fixed proximity speed, then appear once it leaves the 10 m proximity condition.","Best controlled test":"During a controlled hunt with a safe loop and incense, compare speed while the nearest player moves versus stands completely still. The change should be immediate and dramatic. Female name/model is supporting exclusion evidence.","0-evidence identification":"Movement-linked speed is the strongest tell. Also watch for hunt timing around 65% while people are moving close to the ghost.","Common confusion":"Deogen also changes speed by proximity, but it slows because of distance, not player movement, and always knows where you are. Kormos reacts to footsteps but does not use the Dayan 1.2/2.25 fixed pair.","Survival note":"Avoid unnecessary movement near the ghost before hunts. During a hunt, use sight breaks and incense rather than trying to “dance” close to it."}},{"name":"Deildegast","number":4,"page":6,"evidence":["EMF 5","Writing","DOTS"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"DAYL-duh-gahst; approximate Norwegian reading, with a hard g.","origin":"Norway, particularly southern and western traditions; well-attested boundary-stone revenant.","lore":"A person who fraudulently moved a property boundary could return after death, condemned to struggle with the stone and try to restore it. Tales connect the haunting with stolen land and an injustice that remains unsettled. Moving ordinary household props to slow this game ghost is an inventive echo of that boundary-stone motif. [F5]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Starts each hunt attempt at a potential 3.0 m/s, then is slowed by 0.1 m/s for each unique qualifying environmental prop that players have moved or interacted with before that attempt, down to 0.4 m/s after 26 qualifying props. Speed cannot be changed mid-hunt.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Qualifying examples include map throwables and many environment interactions such as switches, taps and the fuse box. Player equipment, cursed possessions and doors do not count. Dead players can help. The counter resets after a completed hunt and after a crucifix blocks a hunt attempt.","Best controlled test":"Before a controlled hunt, move 13+ unique props; the next hunt should be substantially slower than a fresh 3.0 m/s hunt. Repeat after a crucifix burn to see the reset.","0-evidence identification":"A constant, very fast early hunt with no LOS acceleration that becomes deliberately slower after you “trash the map” is highly distinctive.","Common confusion":"Young Thaye is fast without LOS but ages across time, not prop count. Revenant switches based on detection. Moroi speed tracks sanity.","Survival note":"Treat every hunt attempt as a reset. If you need another hunt, weaken it again before provoking one."}},{"name":"Demon","number":5,"page":7,"evidence":["Writing","UV","Freezing"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"DEE-mun.","origin":"Broad religious and literary category; English through Latin from Greek daimōn.","lore":"Ancient Greek daimōn could refer to a divine or intermediary power without necessarily meaning an evil being. Later Christian usage strongly emphasized hostile spirits, temptation and possession. Demon traditions vary across religions; the name does not identify one historical monster with a universal appearance or weakness. [F6]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"One of the earliest hunters. Its normal threshold is 70%, and its ability can initiate a hunt regardless of sanity. The minimum hunt cooldown is about 20 s instead of the standard 25 s.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Incense prevents a regular Demon hunt for only 60 s instead of the usual 90 s. Crucifix effective range is 50% larger: T1 4.5 m, T2 6 m, T3 7.5 m.","Best controlled test":"At safe sanity, a natural hunt is strong evidence. For controlled identification, time from a successful cleanse: a hunt after ~60 s but before ~90 s supports Demon; a crucifix burning noticeably farther away is another clue.","0-evidence identification":"Very early natural hunt + short incense timer + larger crucifix range gives a robust combination. One random early event is not proof.","Common confusion":"Thaye and other early hunters can hunt above 50; cursed hunts invalidate threshold logic.","Survival note":"Place crucifixes early and keep sanity high until evidence is set. Do not assume setup time or “we should be safe” protects you after cursed-item use."}},{"name":"Deogen","number":6,"page":7,"evidence":["DOTS","Writing","Spirit Box"],"forced":"Spirit Box on reduced evidence","pronunciation":"DEE-oh-gen; practical English reading with hard g. Dutch de ogen is roughly duh OH-ghun, with a throaty g.","origin":"Associated online with Belgium’s Sonian Forest; modern legend with uncertain documentary history.","lore":"Retellings describe watching eyes and a mist-like presence in the forest. They often cite a supposed 1937 book and a story of murdered children. That attribution is not securely established by the sources available here; it should not be treated as a verified historical event or an ancient Belgian tradition. “De ogen” means “the eyes.” [F7]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Always knows every player’s location during a hunt. It moves extremely fast while far from its target, then slows to a crawl when very close.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Has a special heavy-breathing Spirit Box response when the player is very close. Spirit Box is guaranteed whenever at least one evidence is available.","Best controlled test":"If footsteps race straight toward your hiding location from across the map, leave the hiding spot before it reaches you and loop a safe object. The dramatic slow-down at close range is conclusive.","0-evidence identification":"Hiding is the test you should NOT complete. Its omniscience plus close-range crawl is unmistakable.","Common confusion":"Revenant is the opposite pattern: slow until it detects a player, then extremely fast.","Survival note":"Never hide once you know/suspect Deogen. Move to a broad loop, keep the ghost close enough to stay slow, and avoid dead ends."}},{"name":"Gallu","number":7,"page":7,"evidence":["EMF 5","UV","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"GAL-loo; an English approximation of an ancient name.","origin":"Ancient Mesopotamia; well-attested underworld demon tradition, related to Sumerian galla and Akkadian gallû.","lore":"These beings act as relentless agents of the underworld. In the Inana and Dumuzi cycle, demons accompany Inana out of the realm of the dead and seek a replacement for her. They represent the underworld’s claim on the living, rather than a modern ghost with a human biography. Precise ancient speech is reconstructed. [F8, F9]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Has three states. Normal is standard. Protective equipment can push it to Enraged; an enraged hunt that ends turns it Weakened. State changes affect threshold, base speed, incense blind time and crucifix range.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Enraged: 60% threshold, ~1.955 m/s, incense blind 4 s, crucifix range −2 m, and it cannot disturb salt. Weakened: 40%, ~1.36 m/s, incense blind 6 s, crucifix range +1 m. Normal: 50%, 1.7 m/s, 5 s blind. Salt/incense/crucifix can drive state transitions.","Best controlled test":"A controlled salt test can be powerful: a normal Gallu may step in salt, then after it enrages it can ignore later piles. Compare hunt speeds across state changes.","0-evidence identification":"Protection-linked state changes are the key. A slow hunt after surviving an enraged hunt is especially telling.","Common confusion":"Wraith never disturbs salt at all; Gallu can in normal/weakened states. Twins/Obambo can vary speeds but are not directly toggled by protection.","Survival note":"Using protection is still worth surviving, but expect a power spike after it takes effect. Plan enough distance to escape after incense wears off."}},{"name":"Goryo","number":8,"page":8,"evidence":["DOTS","EMF 5","UV"],"forced":"DOTS on reduced evidence","pronunciation":"goh-ryoh; Japanese goryō, with a longer final oh. Keep ryo together.","origin":"Japan; well-attested spirit-veneration and pacification traditions.","lore":"Goryō is an honorific name for spirits, especially powerful dead whose resentment was feared to cause disasters or disease. Rites could appease them and transform a dangerous relationship into a protective one. Sugawara no Michizane is a famous example within this wider history. Goryō and onryō overlap culturally; they are not mutually exclusive species. [F10, P3]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Cannot change its favorite room and does not perform long roams. Its DOTS evidence is special: the DOTS silhouette is only visible through a video camera and only while no player is in the ghost’s current room.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"DOTS is guaranteed on reduced-evidence settings with at least one evidence available. On true 0-evidence, that forced evidence is disabled, making a Goryo one of the harder ghosts to prove.","Best controlled test":"Leave the room, watch camera coverage, and look for camera-only DOTS. Compare room stability across a longer contract.","0-evidence identification":"No favorite-room change is supportive but slow to prove. Avoid overclaiming Goryo from “it stayed put” in a short match.","Common confusion":"Many ghosts can randomly stay in the same room for long periods.","Survival note":"Standard survival. Its predictability can help you place crucifix coverage and a safe evidence station."}},{"name":"Hantu","number":9,"page":8,"evidence":["Orbs","UV","Freezing"],"forced":"Freezing on reduced evidence","pronunciation":"HAHN-too; approximate Malay/Indonesian reading.","origin":"Malay-speaking and Indonesian-speaking Southeast Asia; broad, well-attested ghost or spirit term.","lore":"Hantu occurs in names for many different supernatural beings; their appearances, habitats and relationships with people depend on the particular tradition. It is not inherently the name of one cold-weather creature. The game’s temperature- controlled speed therefore belongs to its own design, rather than to a single shared Southeast Asian legend. [F11]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Hunt speed is controlled by the temperature of the room it is currently moving through. It is slow in warm areas and can become very fast below freezing. It does not use standard LOS acceleration.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Emits visible freezing breath from the ghost itself during hunts when the fuse box is off. It cannot turn the fuse box on and is more likely to switch it off. Hantu — dossier details","Best controlled test":"Turn the breaker off and watch a safe hunt from a distance for ghost breath. Compare footsteps as it crosses warm/cold zones.","0-evidence identification":"Temperature-linked constant speed + no LOS acceleration is distinctive.","Common confusion":"Thaye/Deildegast can have fixed speeds but their speed changes are tied to age/props, not room temperature.","Survival note":"Keep breaker on to warm the map when possible. Do not loop a Hantu through a freezing ghost room unless you have a strong escape plan."}},{"name":"Jinn","number":10,"page":9,"evidence":["EMF 5","UV","Freezing"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"JIN; rhymes with pin.","origin":"Arabian and Islamic traditions, with extensive regional traditions beyond Arabia; well-attested.","lore":"Jinn are beings distinct from humans and angels, described in Islamic tradition as created from smokeless fire. They may have moral choice and need not be evil; regional stories include helpful, dangerous and deceptive jinn. The lamp-bound wish granter of popular entertainment is only one much narrower image. Electricity and fuse-box rules are game mechanics. [F12]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"With the fuse box ON, line of sight, and the target more than roughly 3 m away, the Jinn can move at a fixed ~2.5 m/s until it closes distance or conditions break.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Cannot directly turn the fuse box off. It can use an ability tied to the breaker that drains a nearby player’s sanity and produces EMF at/near the fuse box.","Best controlled test":"Compare a hunt with breaker ON versus OFF. A sudden fast approach at range that drops back near you is strong Jinn behavior.","0-evidence identification":"Breaker-dependent speed burst is the key. “Breaker never turned off” alone is weak because RNG may simply not choose it.","Common confusion":"Raiju is fast near active electronics; Hantu speed depends on cold; standard ghosts can build LOS speed gradually rather than snap to Jinn conditions.","Survival note":"Break line of sight and/or turn breaker off before deliberate hunt tests. Avoid long hallways when the breaker is on."}},{"name":"Kormos","number":11,"page":9,"evidence":["Orbs","Spirit Box","UV"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"KOR-moss; practical English reading. Körmös has rounded vowels that English spelling only approximates.","origin":"Altai and related Turkic traditions; attested spirit terminology, but regional meanings and spellings vary.","lore":"Körmös can refer to spirits of the dead and to spirits within shamanic cosmologies; some accounts distinguish different classes and relationships to ancestors or the underworld. It is misleading to turn the term into one universal “Turkish demon.” The claim that the name proves a literally blind, footstep-hunting monster is not a secure folklore identification. [F13]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Nearly blind, but has extreme footstep hearing. During hunts it can hear crouch-walking at about 10 m, walking at ~15 m and sprinting at ~30 m on the same floor. Heard footsteps create waypoints and can speed its approach.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"It normally has no standard LOS, but can visually acquire a moving player within ~5 m with a clear path. Outside hunts, sprinting in the same room can raise its hunt threshold to 70%. It cannot perform mist-form or chasing events. Through- wall and excessive-distance killing were fixed in v0.18.0.0; they are not intended abilities.","Best controlled test":"Stand completely still in a deliberately safe open position with incense and an escape route ready. A Kormos may fail to visually lock onto you like a normal ghost. Move briefly and listen for the response. Close contact remains lethal; do not assume an intended kill-through-wall ability.","0-evidence identification":"Footstep-driven pathing + near blindness is the core tell.","Common confusion":"Yokai is “deaf” to voice/electronics at range, but sees normally. Dayan reacts to movement proximity with fixed speed values rather than footstep hearing.","Survival note":"Do not sprint unless you must. Crouch-walk only when necessary, then stop completely. Pick a location the ghost is unlikely to wander within 1.5 m of."}},{"name":"Mare","number":12,"page":10,"evidence":["Writing","Orbs","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"MAIR; rhymes with care in English.","origin":"Germanic and Scandinavian night-spirit traditions; well-attested, related to the mare in nightmare.","lore":"The mare or mara oppresses sleeping people, sometimes described as riding or pressing on the sleeper and bringing frightening dreams. Related accounts concern troubled horses as well. This is a night-being tradition, not the ordinary English word for a female horse. The game uses the darkness association but supplies its own light-switch behavior. [F14]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Hunts earlier when its current room is dark and later when lights are on. It cannot directly turn lights on.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Can use a distinctive ability to immediately switch a light back off soon after a player switches it on; also has strong affinity for light-bulb breaking events.","Best controlled test":"Repeatedly use room lights near the ghost. An immediate “nope” switch-off is strong Mare evidence; a long contract in which the ghost never turns a light on is only supporting.","0-evidence identification":"Hunt threshold changing with lighting + instant switch-off are the strongest tells.","Common confusion":"Any ghost can turn lights off. Breaker loss can create a dark-map false impression.","Survival note":"Keep the ghost room and escape route lit when possible. Restore breaker and lighting before deliberately lowering sanity."}},{"name":"Moroi","number":13,"page":10,"evidence":["Writing","Freezing","Spirit Box"],"forced":"Spirit Box on reduced evidence","pronunciation":"moh-ROY; Romanian-based, with stress on the last syllable.","origin":"Romania; well-attested, regionally variable ghost and vampire-related traditions.","lore":"Moroi may denote troublesome returning dead; some accounts particularly concern children who died without baptism, while others connect the word with strigoi and harmful supernatural activity. There is no single consistent vampire taxonomy across all Romanian communities. The game’s sanity-linked speed and curse are not historical diagnostic rules. [F15, P4]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Its base hunt speed increases as average team sanity falls. At very low sanity it is one of the fastest possible ghosts once LOS acceleration is added.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"A successful Spirit Box response or relevant paranormal sound can curse the listener, causing substantially faster passive sanity drain even in lit areas. Sanity medication removes the curse. Incense blinds a Moroi for about 7 s rather than 5 s.","Best controlled test":"Track speed across controlled hunts at very different sanity levels. If a player received Spirit Box/paramic evidence and sanity plummets despite light discipline, consider the curse.","0-evidence identification":"Sanity-linked speed is the clean tell; do not confuse normal LOS acceleration with a true base-speed change.","Common confusion":"Thaye changes with time/age, Deildegast with props, Obambo with timed states.","Survival note":"Do not let team sanity collapse. Carry medication and incense; the longer blind duration is useful, but a 0%-sanity Moroi can erase distance rapidly."}},{"name":"Myling","number":14,"page":10,"evidence":["Writing","EMF 5","UV"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"MEE-ling is a rough aid; Swedish y is an ee-like vowel spoken with rounded lips.","origin":"Sweden and wider Scandinavian dead-child traditions; well-attested.","lore":"A myling is commonly the restless ghost of a secretly killed or improperly buried infant. Stories let the child reveal the crime, call for recognition or seek a proper burial; details differ locally. These are tragic tales about concealed death and social obligations, not simply a tradition of unusually quiet adult ghosts. [F16, P5]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Its hunt vocalizations and footsteps are audible from a shorter range than normal—about 12 m versus roughly 20 m for a standard ghost.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Produces paranormal sounds more often, making sound-recording / parabolic work more active.","Best controlled test":"Classic flashlight-interference test: while safely hidden, compare when your held/near electronic interference begins versus when footsteps become audible. With a Myling, interference can begin before you hear it.","0-evidence identification":"Short hunt-audio range is the core behavior tell. Use repeated hunts because walls/floors/audio settings can muddy one sample.","Common confusion":"A quiet ghost or obstructed audio can fake it.","Survival note":"Do not rely on hearing alone to judge distance. Watch electronics/interference and use prepared hiding routes."}},{"name":"Obake","number":15,"page":11,"evidence":["EMF 5","Orbs","UV"],"forced":"UV on reduced evidence","pronunciation":"oh-bah-keh; three Japanese syllables, final eh rather than ay.","origin":"Japan; well-attested broad word associated with transformation, apparitions and uncanny beings.","lore":"Obake and bakemono are connected with changing or transforming. They can describe shapeshifting creatures and, in everyday use, ghosts or monsters more broadly. An obake is not invariably a dead human or a creature with extra fingers. The game’s changing model draws on the transformation theme; its fingerprints are a game-specific clue. [F17, P6]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Has a reduced chance to leave UV evidence on eligible interactions, and its UV marks fade faster. It can leave unique abnormal prints such as a six-finger handprint.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"During a hunt, it can briefly shapeshift into another ghost model for a blink. UV is guaranteed on reduced-evidence modes with at least one evidence.","Best controlled test":"Check every eligible UV interaction carefully and learn the abnormal print patterns. During safe hunt observation, watch for a one-blink model change.","0-evidence identification":"Hunt shapeshift is the conclusive 0-evidence test.","Common confusion":"Missing UV once is not exclusion; Obake is specifically allowed to skip prints more often.","Survival note":"Standard hunt survival. If hunt testing, view from a safe loop/hiding observation angle and prioritize survival over staring at the model."}},{"name":"Obambo","number":16,"page":11,"evidence":["Writing","UV","DOTS"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"oh-BAHM-boh; tentative English reading, not a verified local-language pronunciation.","origin":"Equatorial west-central Africa, especially historical accounts from the Gabon region; attested name with a limited and mediated source record.","lore":"Older accounts use Obambou for a spirit associated with possession or the unsettled dead. One tradition describes a wandering spirit asking relatives for a small dwelling near their home. Colonial writers often imposed words such as “devil” on local beliefs, so these reports need context and should not be generalized to all African cultures. [F18, F19]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Always begins calm. One minute after the exit door is first opened it switches state, then flips every two minutes (community-documented timing). It can change state during a hunt. Calm has high ambient activity but a 10% hunt threshold and slow base speed; aggressive has lower ambient activity but a 65% threshold and fast base speed. Obambo — dossier details","Ability / hidden mechanic":"A hunt that begins while aggressive is 20% shorter, even if the ghost switches state mid-hunt.","Best controlled test":"Time activity phases and hunt speed. A sudden mid-hunt base-speed snap with no obvious LOS cause is strong. On a known map/difficulty, time an aggressive-start hunt for the 20% shorter duration.","0-evidence identification":"Timed two-state cycle + opposite activity/aggression pattern is the signature.","Common confusion":"Twins can produce two hunt base speeds; Gallu also has states; neither flips on Obambo’s timed cycle.","Survival note":"Do not mistake a “quiet” aggressive phase for safety. At 65% it may hunt while barely interacting."}},{"name":"Oni","number":17,"page":12,"evidence":["DOTS","EMF 5","Freezing"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"oh-nee; two Japanese syllables.","origin":"Japan; well-attested, diverse ogre and demon traditions.","lore":"Oni are often pictured as large horned beings carrying clubs and wearing tiger-skin clothing. Stories portray frightening attackers or punishers, while some local traditions allow protective or more sympathetic roles. They are not all ghosts of dead humans. Their strength and imposing appearance are cultural motifs; the game’s visibility and event restrictions are separate. [F20]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"More visible during hunts, spending a greater fraction of its blink cycle visible. It cannot perform the mist/airball ghost event.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Oni ghost events are intense. As of v0.19.0.0, the intended sanity drain on collision during singing manifestations is 20% (the patch fixed a prior 10% bug).","Best controlled test":"Watch hunt blink visibility from safety and note event type history. A ghost that repeatedly manifests but never does an airball supports Oni; one missing event type is not proof.","0-evidence identification":"Very visible hunt blinking + impossible airball event are the core behavior set.","Common confusion":"Phantom is the opposite blink extreme—much longer invisible periods.","Survival note":"Standard survival. Treat frequent manifestations as sanity pressure even if it is not hunting yet."}},{"name":"Onryo","number":18,"page":12,"evidence":["Orbs","Freezing","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"ohn-ryoh; Japanese onryō, with a longer final oh. Keep ryo together.","origin":"Japan; well-attested vengeful-spirit tradition.","lore":"An onryō is a spirit whose grievance or resentment brings harm after death. Historical accounts link such spirits with calamity and efforts at ritual pacification; theater and later horror fiction develop more personal revenge narratives. Both male and female figures occur. The game’s fear of flames and candle-count rules do not define the traditional onryō. [F10, F21, P7]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Lit firelights near the ghost can block hunt attempts, taking priority over crucifixes. A blocked attempt extinguishes a flame. The ghost also tracks extinguished flames; a repeated “third flame” pattern can trigger a hunt at any sanity.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"In practical testing, flames are both protection and a trigger. This creates the classic Onryo candle test: controlled flames near crucifix coverage and careful counting.","Best controlled test":"Set multiple firelights near the ghost with crucifix backup. Track each extinguish and whether a hunt tries to fire on/after the third counted flame. Reset your assumptions after complex cursed-item or room-change behavior.","0-evidence identification":"Consistent flame-block/hunt behavior is the main proof.","Common confusion":"Any ghost can blow out candles; random timing can fake the “third candle” if you do not count carefully.","Survival note":"Never run an Onryo test without crucifix coverage and an escape route. Flames are not unconditional safety."}},{"name":"Phantom","number":19,"page":13,"evidence":["DOTS","UV","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"FAN-tum.","origin":"European literary and ordinary-language term with Greek roots; broad apparition category.","lore":"A phantom is an appearance or specter, often the seeming presence of someone absent or dead. Stories may make such an apparition a warning, a memory or a frightening visitor. The word does not specify one culture’s creature or a consistent behavior pattern. Disappearing in a photograph is a Phasmophobia rule, not a dictionary definition. [F22]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Spends longer invisible during hunt blink cycles, making it look like it “teleports” between visible flashes. Looking at a manifested Phantom drains sanity faster.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"A successful ghost photo during a manifestation makes the Phantom disappear visually while the event can continue; the photo can lack the visible ghost model.","Best controlled test":"Take a clean ghost photo during an event and watch for disappearance. During a hunt, compare its long invisible blink windows against Oni/standard.","0-evidence identification":"Photo disappearance or characteristic blink pattern are strong.","Common confusion":"Oni is unusually visible; bad lighting can make any ghost seem invisible.","Survival note":"Do not stare longer than needed. Hunt by sound/route, not visual tracking alone."}},{"name":"Poltergeist","number":20,"page":13,"evidence":["Writing","UV","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"POHL-ter-gyst; gyst rhymes with heist.","origin":"German term and wider European haunting traditions; well-attested name for a noisy spirit or disturbance.","lore":"Accounts focus on knocks, thrown objects and seemingly unexplained physical disruption. The German components refer to making a racket and to a spirit. Later psychical-research literature also debated explanations other than a dead person’s ghost. The game’s object-throwing theme echoes these accounts, but precise probabilities and sanity effects are invented mechanics. [F3, F23]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Has an exceptionally high relationship with throwable objects and can throw multiple nearby props essentially at once. During hunts it throws environmental objects much more frequently than most ghosts.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Its multi-object throw can drain sanity based on the number of affected objects.","Best controlled test":"Build a “polty pile” of many small throwables in the active room and watch from safety. A simultaneous explosive spread is the classic confirmation.","0-evidence identification":"Multi-throw is highly distinctive when clearly observed.","Common confusion":"High RNG activity can make any ghost seem throw-happy; one thrown cup proves nothing.","Survival note":"Do not loiter at low sanity in a prop-rich room just to farm activity. Set the pile, back off, and observe."}},{"name":"Raiju","number":21,"page":13,"evidence":["DOTS","EMF 5","Orbs"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"rye-joo; Japanese raijū, with a longer final oo.","origin":"Japan; well-attested thunder-beast tradition.","lore":"Raijū are beasts associated with thunderstorms and lightning. Historical depictions vary from small mammal-like creatures to stranger composite forms; there is no single required animal shape. They give lightning an animal presence in story and image. The game extends the storm association into modern electronic equipment, which is a thematic adaptation. [F24, P8]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Active qualifying electronics near the ghost raise its hunt threshold and set a fast hunt speed around 2.5 m/s. Its electronic interference radius is also larger than normal.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"The danger follows electronics in the environment, not just the item in your hand. An evidence station full of powered gear can turn the favorite room into a speed zone.","Best controlled test":"During a controlled hunt, compare footsteps as it enters/leaves the radius of clearly active electronics. You should hear a sharp speed change.","0-evidence identification":"Electronics-linked speed zone + larger interference range is the signature.","Common confusion":"Jinn is fast at range with breaker ON; Moroi/Thaye can simply be fast.","Survival note":"Turn off/drop unnecessary active electronics before provoking hunts and do not hide beside powered equipment."}},{"name":"Revenant","number":22,"page":14,"evidence":["Writing","Orbs","Freezing"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"REV-uh-nunt; usual English reading.","origin":"European returning-dead traditions; English borrowed from French revenant, one who returns.","lore":"Medieval and later stories describe dead people returning, sometimes bodily and sometimes as apparitions, to trouble the living. Unresolved wrongdoing, death and burial can matter, but accounts differ. “Revenant” names the return rather than a fixed ability set; the game’s slow-searching and fast-chasing phases are not a universal folklore pattern. [F3, F25]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Very slow while it has no player detection waypoint, but rockets to about 3.0 m/s after detecting a player through sight, voice or electronics.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"The signature is not just “fast ghost”—it is the enormous switch between slow searching and fast pursuit.","Best controlled test":"Listen from a safe, silent hiding spot. If footsteps are abnormally slow, then become extremely fast after a deliberate distant detection and later slow again, Revenant is very likely.","0-evidence identification":"Slow-to-instant-fast transition is one of the cleanest 0-evidence tells.","Common confusion":"Deogen starts fast toward hidden players then slows close—the opposite. Deildegast can be 3.0 from hunt start but remains constant for that hunt.","Survival note":"Never let it detect you unless you already have distance, incense and a route. Electronics discipline matters enormously."}},{"name":"Shade","number":23,"page":14,"evidence":["Writing","EMF 5","Freezing"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"SHAYD.","origin":"English literary ghost term, used especially of the dead in classical-underworld writing and translation.","lore":"A shade is the shadow-like presence or soul of a dead person. The term evokes an insubstantial continuation of life rather than one named monster. A shade may speak or retain memory in literary accounts; shyness, low activity and reluctance to hunt near people are the game’s specialization of the word. [F26]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Extremely shy. Its interactions and events are strongly suppressed around players, and it cannot initiate a hunt while a player is in the same room as the ghost’s actual current location.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Late natural hunt threshold (~35%) is one of the strongest statistical clues. However, room boundaries and ghost roaming can make “same room” tests look inconsistent.","Best controlled test":"At low sanity, stay in the correctly identified current room while protected and observe whether hunt attempts only occur after the ghost roams out. Use motion/EMF to understand location rather than favorite-room label alone.","0-evidence identification":"Late hunt + persistent player-proximity suppression is the behavior package.","Common confusion":"Any ghost can be passive by RNG. “It did nothing for five minutes” is not a Shade test.","Survival note":"Its late threshold gives more setup time, but do not become complacent once team sanity crosses the mid-30s."}},{"name":"Spirit","number":24,"page":15,"evidence":["Writing","EMF 5","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"SPIR-it.","origin":"Broad cross-cultural concept; English word through Latin spiritus, breath or spirit.","lore":"Spirit can mean a soul, an incorporeal being or a nonhuman supernatural presence depending on the religion and context. Stories of the dead lingering through attachment or unfinished obligations occur in many places, but should not be treated as one universal doctrine. Phasmophobia gives this very broad label a specific evidence combination and incense timer. [F27]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"The baseline ghost with one major hidden mechanic: after a successful incense cleanse outside a hunt, it cannot start a regular hunt for about 180 s instead of the usual 90 s.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Otherwise behaves close to standard mechanics, so it is often identified by exclusion or the incense timer.","Best controlled test":"Cleanse the ghost correctly, start a timer from the actual successful cleanse, and maintain hunt-eligible sanity. If no hunt occurs for roughly three minutes, Spirit rises sharply in probability.","0-evidence identification":"Incense timer is the strongest behavior test.","Common confusion":"Bad cleanse placement can produce a false timer. Demon is the short 60 s opposite.","Survival note":"Standard survival. Use the long post-cleanse safety window to finish objectives rather than waiting in danger."}},{"name":"Thaye","number":25,"page":15,"evidence":["DOTS","Writing","Orbs"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"thuh-YEH; two syllables, th as in thin; Burmese tone is only approximated.","origin":"Myanmar; established Burmese word for ghost, with varied local and Buddhist-influenced traditions.","lore":"Thayé is not one uniquely named individual. Accounts of these ghosts describe frightening or troublesome dead, sometimes linked to illness and unsettled relationships with the living. Appearances and explanations vary, and the word should not be equated with the entire nat tradition. Rapidly aging when investigators remain nearby is the game’s invention. [F28, P9]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Ages when players spend time near it. With age it becomes slower, less active, and hunts at lower and lower sanity. It starts among the fastest and earliest hunters and can end as one of the slowest/least aggressive.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Its age can be queried with the Ouija Board; repeated age answers can confirm progression. It does not gain normal LOS acceleration because its hunt speed is age-defined.","Best controlled test":"Compare an early hunt to a much later hunt after spending time near the ghost. A large, progressive slowdown is classic Thaye.","0-evidence identification":"Time/age-linked progressive slowdown is the signature.","Common confusion":"Obambo flips states back and forth; Deildegast resets per hunt and responds to props. Thaye trends one direction over the contract.","Survival note":"Treat a fresh Thaye like an emergency. Get protection down early; if you survive long enough near it, later hunts become easier."}},{"name":"The Mimic","number":26,"page":15,"evidence":["UV","Freezing","Spirit Box"],"forced":"Extra fake Ghost Orbs even on zero evidence","pronunciation":"thuh MIM-ik.","origin":"English descriptive label used as a game type; imitation is a widespread story motif.","lore":"Doubles, deceivers and shapeshifters appear in many traditions, but that does not establish a single historical ghost species called “The Mimic.” The game makes imitation into a distinct mechanical identity. Its false Orbs and changing copied behaviors are part of that design; a connection to any particular folklore figure needs separate evidence. [F29, G2]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Can imitate the behavior, hunt threshold, speed and abilities of other ghosts, changing mimic target periodically. It does not change type mid-hunt. Ghost Orbs are an extra ability, not one of its three standard evidences, and appear even on 0- evidence settings.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Because it copies behavior, a Mimic can produce “impossible” combinations across a contract: e.g., one hunt looks like a Deogen, a later hunt like a Hantu. It also retains certain internal counters/ongoing effects in ways that can make advanced tests messy.","Best controlled test":"Whenever Orbs are present, keep Mimic open until UV + Freezing + Spirit Box have been checked according to difficulty. On 0 evidence, Orbs themselves are the giveaway because normal evidence is disabled but Mimic Orbs remain.","0-evidence identification":"Ghost Orbs on true 0-evidence = Mimic. In reduced evidence, contradictory changing behavior plus Orbs is suspicious.","Common confusion":"Every ghost—it is designed to impersonate them.","Survival note":"Never rely on one hunt’s behavior as a permanent safety rule. A Mimic can change into a much more dangerous ghost before the next hunt."}},{"name":"The Twins","number":27,"page":16,"evidence":["EMF 5","Freezing","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"thuh TWINZ.","origin":"English descriptive label used as a game type; no single established folklore origin is specified.","lore":"Twin figures can be divine, heroic, ominous or ordinary across different traditions. A generic motif is not evidence that this ghost derives from a particular pair of mythological twins or from a film’s imagery. Phasmophobia’s two-range interactions belong to its fictional type; they do not demonstrate two separate historical spirits. [F30, G2]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Mechanically one ghost with two interaction ranges / “twin” behavior. It can create interactions far apart in a short window and hunts can originate from different effective locations. Hunt base speed is chosen from a slower or faster twin value.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"The speed alternation is moderate—not the extreme Revenant/Deogen type. Activity-monitor “twinteraction” patterns are supporting, not conclusive.","Best controlled test":"Compare several hunts. If one is consistently a little slow and another a little fast at base before LOS buildup, Twins is plausible. Track widely separated interaction timing.","0-evidence identification":"Alternating mild hunt speeds across multiple hunts is the strongest behavior clue.","Common confusion":"Aswang starts around slow-Twin pace; Obambo uses similar two speeds but on a timed state system and can change mid- hunt.","Survival note":"Do not assume the ghost will hunt from the favorite room. Keep routes safe from adjacent/remote interaction areas."}},{"name":"Wraith","number":28,"page":16,"evidence":["DOTS","EMF 5","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"RAYTH; silent w, th as in thin.","origin":"Scots and Scottish literary usage; well-attested apparition term, with uncertain deeper etymology.","lore":"A wraith can be the likeness of a living person seen as a death omen, or an apparition of someone dead. Later fantasy often makes wraiths uniformly sinister, but that is not the whole history of the word. Floating, teleporting and avoiding salt are the game’s particular interpretation. [F3, F31]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Never disturbs salt. It can teleport near a player outside hunts, creating an EMF reading near the destination and potentially causing sudden activity away from the favorite room. Wraith — dossier details","Ability / hidden mechanic":"Salt immunity is a physical mechanic, not a personality tendency. However, an enraged Gallu can also fail to disturb salt, so context matters in the expanded ghost pool.","Best controlled test":"Place salt directly in likely ghost pathing, ideally use motion evidence to confirm it walked through. If it repeatedly crosses without disturbing salt from a neutral state, Wraith is a top suspect.","0-evidence identification":"Verified salt pass-through is the classic test.","Common confusion":"Enraged Gallu can ignore salt temporarily; be aware of recent crucifix/incense/salt state changes.","Survival note":"Do not assume distance from the favorite room means safety; teleport activity can move the ghost close before a hunt."}},{"name":"Yokai","number":29,"page":17,"evidence":["DOTS","Orbs","Spirit Box"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"yoh-kye; Japanese yōkai, with a longer first oh.","origin":"Japan; well-attested umbrella category of uncanny beings and phenomena.","lore":"Yōkai can include shapeshifting animals, animated objects, oni and many other strange presences. The category has changed over time and is not simply a synonym for one species of human ghost. Traditions can be comic, unsettling or dangerous. The game’s sensitivity to nearby speech and poor hunt detection do not describe yōkai as a whole. [F17, F20, P10]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Nearby player voice can raise its hunt threshold dramatically. During hunts it has very poor detection range for voice and active handheld electronics—roughly 2.5 m instead of the much larger normal range. Visual detection remains normal.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"The famous “deaf ghost” test only works if you are out of sight; if it can see you, it will chase normally.","Best controlled test":"From a safe hidden position outside visual LOS, use a controlled active electronic/voice bait at a distance that a normal ghost should detect. If it repeatedly ignores the signal until very close, Yokai is likely.","0-evidence identification":"Short voice/electronics detection is the best tell.","Common confusion":"Kormos has special footstep hearing but near-blind vision; Yokai’s weakness is voice/electronics, not footsteps.","Survival note":"Once hidden and out of sight, stay quiet/off anyway. Do not talk near the ghost at high sanity unless you are intentionally testing."}},{"name":"Yurei","number":30,"page":17,"evidence":["DOTS","Orbs","Freezing"],"forced":"","pronunciation":"yoo-ray; Japanese yūrei, with a longer first oo.","origin":"Japan; well-attested term for spirits of the dead, with changing historical meanings.","lore":"Unresolved attachments and emotions often keep a yūrei connected to the living in stories. The familiar white burial clothing, loose hair and absent feet are conventions of particular artistic and theatrical traditions, not a universal appearance. Vengeful spirits overlap with this category. Door-slamming and measured sanity loss remain game rules. [F21, P11]","fields":{"Core mechanics":"Can use a door-closing ability that fully closes a nearby open door and drains about 15% sanity from players near the ghost. It is one of the few ways the front/exit door can be manipulated outside a hunt/event context.","Ability / hidden mechanic":"A successful incense cleanse also forces a Yurei back to its favorite room and prevents it from roaming out for about 90 s, though events can still occur elsewhere.","Best controlled test":"Watch for a distinct full door slam/close paired with sanity loss and no event. After cleansing, use motion/room activity to test temporary confinement.","0-evidence identification":"Door ability is the strongest tell, but it is RNG-gated—lack of a slam is not exclusion.","Common confusion":"Normal ghosts touch doors constantly; only the specific full-close + sanity-drain behavior is meaningful.","Survival note":"Standard survival. Use the post-incense confinement to stabilize evidence equipment and complete room-based tests."}}],"sections":[{"id":1,"title":"Core Mechanics & Hunt Survival","page":2,"lastPage":3,"text":"EDITION STATUS: Revised October 29, 2026. Released mechanics baseline v0.19.1.0; reported October 21 PS5 cosmetic fix noted\nseparately. Resonance is announced for late November and is not live in this edition. Event weather and Custom settings can\nchange baseline assumptions. See sections 18–19.\nGhost room vs. ghost location\nThe favorite/ghost room is the ghost’s anchor, not a cage. Most ghosts can roam, interact from nearby positions, event elsewhere and start a\nhunt from wherever the ghost is physically standing.\nTemperatures follow where the ghost has been; a room that was cold can lag behind a roaming ghost. Orbs are tied to the favorite room,\nwhich makes them useful for relocating the anchor after confusion.\nGoryo is the major room-stability exception: it cannot change favorite room and cannot perform long roams. Yurei can be temporarily\nconfined by incense.\nNell’s Diner has a special Cooler Room temperature behavior that can look “ghost-cold”; do not call Freezing until a thermometer confirms\n≤ 0°C / 32°F.\nSanity & hunt thresholds\nSituation                                                    Rule / practical use\nStandard baseline             Most ghosts can naturally begin hunting when average team sanity is at or below ~50%.\nBanshee                      Uses its selected target’s sanity while that target is inside.\nEarly hunters               Demon ~70% + rare any-sanity ability; Dayan 65% with moving player near; Raiju 65% near active electronics; Mare\n60% in darkness; Onryo ~60%; Gallu 60% enraged; Obambo 65% aggressive; young Thaye ~75%; Kormos can rise\nto 70% after same-room sprinting.\nLate hunters                  Shade ~35%; Deogen 40%; Mare 40% in lit room; Dayan 45% with still player near; Gallu 40% weakened; old Thaye\ncan fall toward ~15%; calm Obambo 10%.\nCursed hunt                   Cursed possessions can bypass ordinary sanity logic; after a cursed hunt, future hunts remain longer for the\ncontract.\nCustom                           Starting sanity, evidence count, ghost speed, player speed, grace and many other values can invalidate “standard”\nassumptions.\nHow a hunt starts\nExit doors lock at the beginning of the grace period. The ghost is invisible and stationary during normal grace, then begins its hunting phase.\nPreset grace: Amateur 5 s, Intermediate 4 s, Professional 3 s, Nightmare 2 s, Insanity 2 s. A cursed hunt has 1 s grace unless a custom setting\nchanges the contract.\nHunts begin from the ghost’s physical position, not necessarily the favorite room. The Twins have special hunt-origin behavior.\nAfter a normal hunt ends, most ghosts need at least ~25 s before another natural attempt; Demon can attempt after ~20 s.\nCrucifix prevents an attempt only if the ghost is in its effective same-floor range when it tries to start. It does not stop a hunt already in\nprogress.\nHunt duration\nPreset group                           Small                  Medium                     Large\nAmateur (Low)                            15 s                        30 s                        40 s\nIntermediate (Medium)                     20 s                        40 s                        50 s\nProfessional / Nightmare / Insanity (High)      30 s                        50 s                        60 s\nAfter first cursed hunt                    Add 20 s to subsequent hunts  Add 20 s                  Add 20 s\nin that contract\nObambo aggressive-start hunt             20% shorter than the        20% shorter               20% shorter\notherwise applicable duration\nNIGHTMARE/INSANITY: If “kills extend hunts” is active, a player death can add substantial time. Do not leave hiding merely because\nthe hunt has already felt long.\nGhost detection during hunts\nSight: most ghosts have 360° visual detection and gradually accelerate while maintaining line of sight. Repeated corners/doors/furniture that\nfully break sight are more valuable than raw sprint distance.\nVoice: a live microphone can reveal you inside the ghost’s normal detection range. Mute/stop speaking before hiding.\nElectronics: powered handheld electronics can reveal your location. Switch them off before entering the hiding spot; dropping an active item\ncan also create a dangerous beacon in the area.\nExceptions matter: Yokai has very short voice/electronics detection; Kormos is driven heavily by footsteps; Deogen always knows where\neveryone is; Raiju gains speed around active electronics; Myling is heard from shorter range.\nStandard survival sequence\n1. BEFORE hunt range: identify at least two escape paths and physically confirm which official hiding spots are available.\n2. At hunt start: decide “event or hunt” by door lock/electronic interference/light behavior; do not freeze in the ghost room waiting for\ncertainty.\n3. Turn OFF powered handheld electronics and stop talking. Do this before entering the hiding spot.\n4. Break line of sight at least once, preferably repeatedly. Do not let the ghost watch you enter your hiding location.\n5. Close/hold hiding doors as appropriate. Stay silent until you have a reliable end-of-hunt cue.\n6. If detected while exposed: use corners and furniture, save sprint for closing distance to a sight break, and incense before the ghost\nreaches kill range.\n7. Deogen exception: do not hide. Kormos exception: excessive movement/footsteps can be the problem. Aswang exception: correct\nofficial hiding can end a hunt.\nEvents vs. hunts: fast checks\nSignal                            Ghost event                                        Hunt\nFront/exit door        May close, but not locked by a normal event                 Locks at grace-period start\nRoom lights             Event may switch/shatter/flicker locally                Map lights flicker/interfere near ghost; switches generally\ncannot be toggled while hunt is active\nElectronics            Can interfere during nearby manifestation                   Interference follows hunting ghost; dangerous because\npowered handheld gear can be detected\nGhost                  Often appears/approaches as manifestation                  Alternates visible/invisible blink and actively pathfinds for\nplayers\nYour response          Use for photo/video/sound if safe                            Survive first; evidence second","status":"baseline"},{"id":2,"title":"Evidence Deep Dive","page":3,"lastPage":4,"text":"NEGATIVE EVIDENCE RULE: On 3-evidence settings, a properly executed negative can rule ghosts out. On\nNightmare/Insanity/reduced evidence, missing evidence can be intentionally hidden unless it is a forced evidence. On 0 evidence, all\nstandard evidence is hidden; Mimic Orbs still appear.\nEvidence                           Correct collection                               Important traps / special cases\nEMF Level 5           Fresh EMF 2/3-eligible interactions have a 33% chance to display  Re-test multiple fresh interactions; hunts cause\nEMF 5 if EMF 5 is available. Activity monitor “5 point jumps” are    interference.\nnot proof.\nDOTS               Ghost can enter a DOTS state briefly; cover the actual active      Goryo DOTS is camera-only and requires no player in the\narea.                                                             ghost’s current room.\nUltraviolet           Check fresh door/window/switch/keyboard touches and salt     Obake can skip UV prints more often and can leave unique\nfootprints. Charge prints with UV before photo.                     prints; absence from one touch is not enough.\nFreezing            Thermometer reading ≤0°C / 32°F.                                  Visible breath alone is not evidence; Hantu has forced\nfreezing on reduced evidence.\nGhost Orbs          Use night vision in the favorite room; scan from multiple        Mimic Orbs are extra behavior and persist on 0 evidence.\nangles/rooms if the ghost room may have changed.\nGhost Writing        Open/place book inside range. If Writing is available, attempts   A thrown book without writing is meaningful only when\noccur periodically; higher tiers improve success chance/range.    writing is not hidden by difficulty.\nSpirit Box          Same room as ghost, within tier range, room lights off; ask      Deogen and Moroi have forced Spirit Box on 1–2 evidence;\nrecognized questions or use text mode.                     Deogen can give heavy-breath special response.\nForced / special evidence\nGhost                                                               Special rule\nGoryo                  DOTS guaranteed whenever at least 1 evidence is enabled.\nHantu                        Freezing guaranteed whenever at least 1 evidence is enabled.\nMoroi                              Spirit Box guaranteed whenever at least 1 evidence is enabled.\nObake                  UV guaranteed whenever at least 1 evidence is enabled.\nDeogen                           Spirit Box guaranteed whenever at least 1 evidence is enabled.\nGhost                                                               Special rule\nThe Mimic                  Fake Ghost Orbs always appear, including 0-evidence. Its three real evidences are not individually forced on reduced-\nevidence settings.","status":"baseline"},{"id":3,"title":"Master Ghost Matrix","page":4,"lastPage":4,"text":"HOW TO SCAN: Start with evidence, then sanity threshold, then base hunt speed. “Best tell” is in the dossier section; this matrix is\nmeant to eliminate 80% of choices at a glance.\nGhost                     Evidence                  Forced/special         Natural hunt threshold                Base / special speed\nAswang             DOTS • Freezing • Writing      —                50%                              1.53 m/s base → 2.53 max with fast LOS accel.\nBanshee            DOTS • Orbs • UV          —                     Target sanity 50%                  1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nDayan              EMF 5 • Orbs • Spirit Box       —                45% still nearby / 65% moving     1.2 still-near / 2.25 moving-near / standard\nnearby / 50% otherwise           outside 10 m\nDeildegast           EMF 5 • Writing • DOTS       —                50%                               3.0 → 0.4 m/s based on moved map props; no\nLOS accel.\nDemon                 Writing • UV • Freezing       —                   70%; rare ability can hunt at any   1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nsanity\nDeogen             DOTS • Writing • Spirit Box              Spirit Box on 1–2      40%                              ~3.0 far → ~0.4 very close\nevidence\nGallu              EMF 5 • UV • Spirit Box        —                40% weakened / 50% normal /     1.36 / 1.70 / 1.955 m/s + normal LOS\n60% enraged\nGoryo              DOTS • EMF 5 • UV               DOTS on 1–2 evidence  50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nHantu                Orbs • UV • Freezing                 Freezing on 1–2      50%                              ~1.4 warm → ~2.7 freezing; no LOS accel.\nevidence\nJinn               EMF 5 • UV • Freezing        —                50%                               1.7 base; 2.5 at range with breaker ON + LOS\nKormos               Orbs • Spirit Box • UV        —                   50%; 70% after same-room sprint  1.7 base; ~2.21 while pursuing heard footsteps;\ntrigger                        complex LOS\nMare                   Writing • Orbs • Spirit Box      —                60% dark / 40% lit                  1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nMoroi                   Writing • Freezing • Spirit Box           Spirit Box on 1–2      50%                              ~1.5 at high sanity → ~2.25 at 0%; LOS can push\nevidence                                                 ~3.7\nMyling                  Writing • EMF 5 • UV         —                50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nObake             EMF 5 • Orbs • UV               UV on 1–2 evidence    50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nObambo                Writing • UV • DOTS         —                10% calm / 65% aggressive        1.445 calm / 1.955 aggressive + normal LOS\nOni                DOTS • EMF 5 • Freezing       —                50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nOnryo                Orbs • Freezing • Spirit Box     —                 ~60% normally; flame              1.7 m/s + normal LOS\ninteractions modify hunt logic\nPhantom            DOTS • UV • Spirit Box        —                50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nPoltergeist              Writing • UV • Spirit Box       —                50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nRaiju               DOTS • EMF 5 • Orbs         —                50% normally / 65% near           1.7 base / ~2.5 near active electronics\nqualifying active electronics\nRevenant               Writing • Orbs • Freezing       —                50%                              ~1.0 searching → ~3.0 after detecting a player\nShade                  Writing • EMF 5 • Freezing      —                35%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nSpirit                   Writing • EMF 5 • Spirit Box      —                50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nThaye              DOTS • Writing • Orbs        —                 ~75% young → ~15% old          ~2.75 young → ~1.0 old; fixed by age\nThe Mimic           UV • Freezing • Spirit Box + fake      Fake Orbs always,      Copies current ghost behavior     Copies current ghost behavior\nOrbs                            even 0 evidence\nThe Twins           EMF 5 • Freezing • Spirit Box     —                50%                             ~1.53 slow hunt / ~1.87 fast hunt + normal LOS\nWraith              DOTS • EMF 5 • Spirit Box      —                50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS\nYokai              DOTS • Orbs • Spirit Box       —                50% normally; up to ~80% when   1.7 m/s + normal LOS\ntalking very near it\nYurei               DOTS • Orbs • Freezing       —                50%                               1.7 m/s + normal LOS","status":"baseline"},{"id":4,"title":"30 Ghost Dossiers","page":4,"lastPage":17,"text":"Pronunciations are practical English reading aids, not official Kinetic recordings. Capitals mark approximate stress; Japanese vowel\nlength and pitch, Burmese tone and other language-specific sounds cannot be reproduced exactly in English. Where a local reading is\nuncertain, it is labeled. Cultural origin describes the name or tradition, not a confirmed developer statement of inspiration. Folklore\nparagraphs describe beliefs and stories, never evidence for identifying a game ghost. F and P references are listed in section 17.\nCONFIDENCE LADDER: CONCLUSIVE = a mechanical behavior that cannot normally belong to another ghost under the same\nconditions. STRONG = highly distinctive but can be confused by settings/state. SUPPORTING = personality/activity clues only. Build\nanswers from at least one strong/conclusive tell plus evidence when possible.\n1. Aswang\nPronunciation ahs-WAHNG; Tagalog-based, with ng as in sing. Cebuano often stresses the first syllable.\nCultural origin Philippines; well-attested regional traditions, with several different creature types.\nTraditional lore Aswang is an umbrella name for beings described as shapeshifters, witches, corpse eaters, vampires or viscera eaters.\nSome appear human by day and prey on people at night. The versions differ by language and locality; the manananggal is only one\nrelated form. A single universal aswang biography would flatten that diversity. [F1, P1]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • Freezing • Writing                   50%                            1.53 m/s base → 2.53 max with fast LOS accel.     HIGH if caught in\nopen LOS\nAswang — dossier details\nCore mechanics          Lower base speed, but stronger line-of-sight acceleration than a standard ghost: roughly 0.075× base speed per second\ninstead of 0.05×, reaching max speed in about 8.7 s. After its official hiding-spot weakness ends a hunt, it can move toward\nthe remembered hide during the next grace period; this is not a generic zero-grace rule.\nAbility / hidden mechanic    If an Aswang reaches a correctly hidden player in an available official hiding spot, the hunt ends instead of killing that\nplayer. It places a waypoint there at the start of the next hunt and can move toward it during grace. Change hides\nafterward. Community-documented behavior; see section 18.\nBest controlled test       Use an official closet/locker/furniture hiding zone that is actually enabled by the hiding-place setting. Unofficial “safe spots”\ndo not count. During an exposed test, listen for a slightly slow opening pace that ramps unusually quickly while it maintains\nsight.\n0-evidence identification    Official hiding-spot survival + unusually rapid LOS acceleration are the key 0-evidence tells. Hiding-place setting = None\ndisables the special weakness.\nCommon confusion        Slow Twin / calm Obambo can resemble its base pace; a standard ghost can look fast after enough LOS. Test multiple hunts\nand watch whether the base speed itself changes (Aswang does not).\nSurvival note              Break sight early. If you have a known official hiding spot, use it correctly; if it ends the hunt, change your plan before the\nnext one.\n2. Banshee\nPronunciation BAN-shee; usual English pronunciation.\nCultural origin Ireland; well-attested death-messenger tradition; from Irish bean sí, a woman of the fairy mound.\nTraditional lore Her crying or keening foretells or accompanies a death, often within a particular family. She is commonly a lamenting\nmessenger rather than the killer herself. Descriptions of her age and appearance vary. The game’s pursuit of one selected victim is a\ngameplay adaptation of the association with individuals and families. [F2, F3]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • Orbs • UV                                Target sanity 50%                1.7 m/s + normal LOS                                Variable; lethal focus\nin multiplayer\nBanshee — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Chooses a hunt target in multiplayer. While that target is inside the investigation area, the Banshee uses the target’s sanity\nfor hunt checks and ignores other players as kill targets. It has an increased preference for singing ghost events.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Can perform a distinctive Banshee scream detected with the Parabolic Microphone / Sound Recorder. A singing-event\ncollision drains more sanity from the target than a normal event.\nBest controlled test         In multiplayer, compare who is consistently followed or threatened. Scan paranormal sounds repeatedly for the scream. If a\nhunt passes through non-target teammates while the target is inside, that is extremely strong Banshee behavior.\n0-evidence identification   Unique scream is the cleanest behavior confirmation. Target logic can identify it, but do not manufacture dangerous tests\nunless the team understands the mechanic.\nCommon confusion       Phantom also roams toward players; random pathing can fake “following.” The scream is far stronger than personality.\nSurvival note                      If you believe you are the target, play conservatively and carry protection. Non-targets can perform controlled hunt\nobservations while the target remains safely positioned.\n3. Dayan\nPronunciation DAA-yun; Hindi-based, two syllables, not DAY-an.\nCultural origin South Asia, especially Hindi-speaking traditions; established witch term with regional variations.\nTraditional lore A dayan or daayan is a female witch or dangerous supernatural woman in stories, often associated with harmful magic.\nSome narratives describe predation, disguise or an outwardly ordinary woman. These are beliefs and storytelling motifs, not a factual\ncategory of women. The word’s history should not be reduced to a single modern film portrayal. [F4, P2]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nEMF 5 • Orbs • Spirit Box                   45% still nearby / 65% moving    1.2 still-near / 2.25 moving-near / standard outside  VERY HIGH when\nnearby / 50% otherwise         10 m                                              players move near it\nDayan — dossier details\nCore mechanics            Female-only ghost. Its behavior depends on the closest player within 10 m, including a player on another floor. If that player\nis still, hunt threshold is 45% and hunt speed is fixed at 1.2 m/s. If that player is moving, threshold is 65% and speed is fixed\nat 2.25 m/s. With nobody within 10 m it behaves at standard 50% threshold and standard speed rules.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  LOS acceleration can accumulate “in the background” while the Dayan is near a player and using its fixed proximity speed,\nthen appear once it leaves the 10 m proximity condition.\nBest controlled test        During a controlled hunt with a safe loop and incense, compare speed while the nearest player moves versus stands\ncompletely still. The change should be immediate and dramatic. Female name/model is supporting exclusion evidence.\n0-evidence identification   Movement-linked speed is the strongest tell. Also watch for hunt timing around 65% while people are moving close to the\nghost.\nCommon confusion       Deogen also changes speed by proximity, but it slows because of distance, not player movement, and always knows where\nyou are. Kormos reacts to footsteps but does not use the Dayan 1.2/2.25 fixed pair.\nSurvival note              Avoid unnecessary movement near the ghost before hunts. During a hunt, use sight breaks and incense rather than trying\nto “dance” close to it.\n4. Deildegast\nPronunciation DAYL-duh-gahst; approximate Norwegian reading, with a hard g.\nCultural origin Norway, particularly southern and western traditions; well-attested boundary-stone revenant.\nTraditional lore A person who fraudulently moved a property boundary could return after death, condemned to struggle with the stone\nand try to restore it. Tales connect the haunting with stolen land and an injustice that remains unsettled. Moving ordinary household\nprops to slow this game ghost is an inventive echo of that boundary-stone motif. [F5]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nEMF 5 • Writing • DOTS                     50%                              3.0 → 0.4 m/s based on moved map props; no LOS  EXTREME if a hunt\naccel.                                                  starts before you\nweaken it\nDeildegast — dossier details\nCore mechanics             Starts each hunt attempt at a potential 3.0 m/s, then is slowed by 0.1 m/s for each unique qualifying environmental prop\nthat players have moved or interacted with before that attempt, down to 0.4 m/s after 26 qualifying props. Speed cannot be\nchanged mid-hunt.\nAbility / hidden mechanic   Qualifying examples include map throwables and many environment interactions such as switches, taps and the fuse box.\nPlayer equipment, cursed possessions and doors do not count. Dead players can help. The counter resets after a completed\nhunt and after a crucifix blocks a hunt attempt.\nBest controlled test        Before a controlled hunt, move 13+ unique props; the next hunt should be substantially slower than a fresh 3.0 m/s hunt.\nRepeat after a crucifix burn to see the reset.\n0-evidence identification   A constant, very fast early hunt with no LOS acceleration that becomes deliberately slower after you “trash the map” is\nhighly distinctive.\nCommon confusion       Young Thaye is fast without LOS but ages across time, not prop count. Revenant switches based on detection. Moroi speed\ntracks sanity.\nSurvival note               Treat every hunt attempt as a reset. If you need another hunt, weaken it again before provoking one.\n5. Demon\nPronunciation DEE-mun.\nCultural origin Broad religious and literary category; English through Latin from Greek daimōn.\nTraditional lore Ancient Greek daimōn could refer to a divine or intermediary power without necessarily meaning an evil being. Later\nChristian usage strongly emphasized hostile spirits, temptation and possession. Demon traditions vary across religions; the name does\nnot identify one historical monster with a universal appearance or weakness. [F6]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • UV • Freezing                        70%; rare ability can hunt at any  1.7 m/s + normal LOS                        EXTREME early-hunt\nsanity                                                                               threat\nDemon — dossier details\nCore mechanics         One of the earliest hunters. Its normal threshold is 70%, and its ability can initiate a hunt regardless of sanity. The minimum\nhunt cooldown is about 20 s instead of the standard 25 s.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Incense prevents a regular Demon hunt for only 60 s instead of the usual 90 s. Crucifix effective range is 50% larger: T1 4.5\nm, T2 6 m, T3 7.5 m.\nBest controlled test        At safe sanity, a natural hunt is strong evidence. For controlled identification, time from a successful cleanse: a hunt after\n~60 s but before ~90 s supports Demon; a crucifix burning noticeably farther away is another clue.\n0-evidence identification   Very early natural hunt + short incense timer + larger crucifix range gives a robust combination. One random early event is\nnot proof.\nCommon confusion        Thaye and other early hunters can hunt above 50; cursed hunts invalidate threshold logic.\nSurvival note               Place crucifixes early and keep sanity high until evidence is set. Do not assume setup time or “we should be safe” protects\nyou after cursed-item use.\n6. Deogen\nPronunciation DEE-oh-gen; practical English reading with hard g. Dutch de ogen is roughly duh OH-ghun, with a throaty g.\nCultural origin Associated online with Belgium’s Sonian Forest; modern legend with uncertain documentary history.\nTraditional lore Retellings describe watching eyes and a mist-like presence in the forest. They often cite a supposed 1937 book and a\nstory of murdered children. That attribution is not securely established by the sources available here; it should not be treated as a\nverified historical event or an ancient Belgian tradition. “De ogen” means “the eyes.” [F7]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • Writing • Spirit Box | Forced: Spirit Box   40%                            ~3.0 far → ~0.4 very close                        Deadly if you hide;\non 1–2 evidence                                                                                                  manageable if you\nloop\nDeogen — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Always knows every player’s location during a hunt. It moves extremely fast while far from its target, then slows to a crawl\nwhen very close.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Has a special heavy-breathing Spirit Box response when the player is very close. Spirit Box is guaranteed whenever at least\none evidence is available.\nBest controlled test            If footsteps race straight toward your hiding location from across the map, leave the hiding spot before it reaches you and\nloop a safe object. The dramatic slow-down at close range is conclusive.\n0-evidence identification   Hiding is the test you should NOT complete. Its omniscience plus close-range crawl is unmistakable.\nCommon confusion        Revenant is the opposite pattern: slow until it detects a player, then extremely fast.\nSurvival note             Never hide once you know/suspect Deogen. Move to a broad loop, keep the ghost close enough to stay slow, and avoid\ndead ends.\n7. Gallu\nPronunciation GAL-loo; an English approximation of an ancient name.\nCultural origin Ancient Mesopotamia; well-attested underworld demon tradition, related to Sumerian galla and Akkadian gallû.\nTraditional lore These beings act as relentless agents of the underworld. In the Inana and Dumuzi cycle, demons accompany Inana out\nof the realm of the dead and seek a replacement for her. They represent the underworld’s claim on the living, rather than a modern\nghost with a human biography. Precise ancient speech is reconstructed. [F8, F9]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nEMF 5 • UV • Spirit Box                     40% weakened / 50% normal /    1.36 / 1.70 / 1.955 m/s + normal LOS              HIGH; protection can\n60% enraged                                                     make it stronger\nGallu — dossier details\nCore mechanics          Has three states. Normal is standard. Protective equipment can push it to Enraged; an enraged hunt that ends turns it\nWeakened. State changes affect threshold, base speed, incense blind time and crucifix range.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Enraged: 60% threshold, ~1.955 m/s, incense blind 4 s, crucifix range −2 m, and it cannot disturb salt. Weakened: 40%, ~1.36\nm/s, incense blind 6 s, crucifix range +1 m. Normal: 50%, 1.7 m/s, 5 s blind. Salt/incense/crucifix can drive state transitions.\nBest controlled test      A controlled salt test can be powerful: a normal Gallu may step in salt, then after it enrages it can ignore later piles.\nCompare hunt speeds across state changes.\n0-evidence identification   Protection-linked state changes are the key. A slow hunt after surviving an enraged hunt is especially telling.\nCommon confusion        Wraith never disturbs salt at all; Gallu can in normal/weakened states. Twins/Obambo can vary speeds but are not directly\ntoggled by protection.\nSurvival note              Using protection is still worth surviving, but expect a power spike after it takes effect. Plan enough distance to escape after\nincense wears off.\n8. Goryo\nPronunciation goh-ryoh; Japanese goryō, with a longer final oh. Keep ryo together.\nCultural origin Japan; well-attested spirit-veneration and pacification traditions.\nTraditional lore Goryō is an honorific name for spirits, especially powerful dead whose resentment was feared to cause disasters or\ndisease. Rites could appease them and transform a dangerous relationship into a protective one. Sugawara no Michizane is a famous\nexample within this wider history. Goryō and onryō overlap culturally; they are not mutually exclusive species. [F10, P3]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • EMF 5 • UV | Forced: DOTS on 1–2       50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                           Standard hunt\nevidence                                                                                                                    danger; difficult\nbehavior ID\nGoryo — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Cannot change its favorite room and does not perform long roams. Its DOTS evidence is special: the DOTS silhouette is only\nvisible through a video camera and only while no player is in the ghost’s current room.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  DOTS is guaranteed on reduced-evidence settings with at least one evidence available. On true 0-evidence, that forced\nevidence is disabled, making a Goryo one of the harder ghosts to prove.\nBest controlled test        Leave the room, watch camera coverage, and look for camera-only DOTS. Compare room stability across a longer contract.\n0-evidence identification  No favorite-room change is supportive but slow to prove. Avoid overclaiming Goryo from “it stayed put” in a short match.\nCommon confusion       Many ghosts can randomly stay in the same room for long periods.\nSurvival note              Standard survival. Its predictability can help you place crucifix coverage and a safe evidence station.\n9. Hantu\nPronunciation HAHN-too; approximate Malay/Indonesian reading.\nCultural origin Malay-speaking and Indonesian-speaking Southeast Asia; broad, well-attested ghost or spirit term.\nTraditional lore Hantu occurs in names for many different supernatural beings; their appearances, habitats and relationships with\npeople depend on the particular tradition. It is not inherently the name of one cold-weather creature. The game’s temperature-\ncontrolled speed therefore belongs to its own design, rather than to a single shared Southeast Asian legend. [F11]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nOrbs • UV • Freezing | Forced: Freezing on 1–2   50%                            ~1.4 warm → ~2.7 freezing; no LOS accel.         HIGH in cold rooms\nevidence\nHantu — dossier details\nCore mechanics          Hunt speed is controlled by the temperature of the room it is currently moving through. It is slow in warm areas and can\nbecome very fast below freezing. It does not use standard LOS acceleration.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Emits visible freezing breath from the ghost itself during hunts when the fuse box is off. It cannot turn the fuse box on and\nis more likely to switch it off.\nHantu — dossier details\nBest controlled test       Turn the breaker off and watch a safe hunt from a distance for ghost breath. Compare footsteps as it crosses warm/cold\nzones.\n0-evidence identification   Temperature-linked constant speed + no LOS acceleration is distinctive.\nCommon confusion        Thaye/Deildegast can have fixed speeds but their speed changes are tied to age/props, not room temperature.\nSurvival note             Keep breaker on to warm the map when possible. Do not loop a Hantu through a freezing ghost room unless you have a\nstrong escape plan.\n10. Jinn\nPronunciation JIN; rhymes with pin.\nCultural origin Arabian and Islamic traditions, with extensive regional traditions beyond Arabia; well-attested.\nTraditional lore Jinn are beings distinct from humans and angels, described in Islamic tradition as created from smokeless fire. They\nmay have moral choice and need not be evil; regional stories include helpful, dangerous and deceptive jinn. The lamp-bound wish\ngranter of popular entertainment is only one much narrower image. Electricity and fuse-box rules are game mechanics. [F12]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nEMF 5 • UV • Freezing                      50%                              1.7 base; 2.5 at range with breaker ON + LOS      HIGH on long sight\nlines\nJinn — dossier details\nCore mechanics           With the fuse box ON, line of sight, and the target more than roughly 3 m away, the Jinn can move at a fixed ~2.5 m/s until it\ncloses distance or conditions break.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Cannot directly turn the fuse box off. It can use an ability tied to the breaker that drains a nearby player’s sanity and\nproduces EMF at/near the fuse box.\nBest controlled test       Compare a hunt with breaker ON versus OFF. A sudden fast approach at range that drops back near you is strong Jinn\nbehavior.\n0-evidence identification   Breaker-dependent speed burst is the key. “Breaker never turned off” alone is weak because RNG may simply not choose it.\nCommon confusion         Raiju is fast near active electronics; Hantu speed depends on cold; standard ghosts can build LOS speed gradually rather\nthan snap to Jinn conditions.\nSurvival note              Break line of sight and/or turn breaker off before deliberate hunt tests. Avoid long hallways when the breaker is on.\n11. Kormos\nPronunciation KOR-moss; practical English reading. Körmös has rounded vowels that English spelling only approximates.\nCultural origin Altai and related Turkic traditions; attested spirit terminology, but regional meanings and spellings vary.\nTraditional lore Körmös can refer to spirits of the dead and to spirits within shamanic cosmologies; some accounts distinguish different\nclasses and relationships to ancestors or the underworld. It is misleading to turn the term into one universal “Turkish demon.” The claim\nthat the name proves a literally blind, footstep-hunting monster is not a secure folklore identification. [F13]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nOrbs • Spirit Box • UV                         50%; 70% after same-room       1.7 base; ~2.21 while pursuing heard footsteps;    VERY HIGH if you\nsprint trigger                 complex LOS                                     panic-run\nKormos — dossier details\nCore mechanics            Nearly blind, but has extreme footstep hearing. During hunts it can hear crouch-walking at about 10 m, walking at ~15 m\nand sprinting at ~30 m on the same floor. Heard footsteps create waypoints and can speed its approach.\nAbility / hidden mechanic    It normally has no standard LOS, but can visually acquire a moving player within ~5 m with a clear path. Outside hunts,\nsprinting in the same room can raise its hunt threshold to 70%. It cannot perform mist-form or chasing events. Through-\nwall and excessive-distance killing were fixed in v0.18.0.0; they are not intended abilities.\nBest controlled test        Stand completely still in a deliberately safe open position with incense and an escape route ready. A Kormos may fail to\nvisually lock onto you like a normal ghost. Move briefly and listen for the response. Close contact remains lethal; do not\nassume an intended kill-through-wall ability.\n0-evidence identification   Footstep-driven pathing + near blindness is the core tell.\nCommon confusion        Yokai is “deaf” to voice/electronics at range, but sees normally. Dayan reacts to movement proximity with fixed speed values\nrather than footstep hearing.\nSurvival note           Do not sprint unless you must. Crouch-walk only when necessary, then stop completely. Pick a location the ghost is unlikely\nto wander within 1.5 m of.\n12. Mare\nPronunciation MAIR; rhymes with care in English.\nCultural origin Germanic and Scandinavian night-spirit traditions; well-attested, related to the mare in nightmare.\nTraditional lore The mare or mara oppresses sleeping people, sometimes described as riding or pressing on the sleeper and bringing\nfrightening dreams. Related accounts concern troubled horses as well. This is a night-being tradition, not the ordinary English word for a\nfemale horse. The game uses the darkness association but supplies its own light-switch behavior. [F14]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • Orbs • Spirit Box                  60% dark / 40% lit                1.7 m/s + normal LOS                          High in dark\ninvestigations\nMare — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Hunts earlier when its current room is dark and later when lights are on. It cannot directly turn lights on.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Can use a distinctive ability to immediately switch a light back off soon after a player switches it on; also has strong affinity\nfor light-bulb breaking events.\nBest controlled test        Repeatedly use room lights near the ghost. An immediate “nope” switch-off is strong Mare evidence; a long contract in\nwhich the ghost never turns a light on is only supporting.\n0-evidence identification   Hunt threshold changing with lighting + instant switch-off are the strongest tells.\nCommon confusion       Any ghost can turn lights off. Breaker loss can create a dark-map false impression.\nSurvival note             Keep the ghost room and escape route lit when possible. Restore breaker and lighting before deliberately lowering sanity.\n13. Moroi\nPronunciation moh-ROY; Romanian-based, with stress on the last syllable.\nCultural origin Romania; well-attested, regionally variable ghost and vampire-related traditions.\nTraditional lore Moroi may denote troublesome returning dead; some accounts particularly concern children who died without\nbaptism, while others connect the word with strigoi and harmful supernatural activity. There is no single consistent vampire taxonomy\nacross all Romanian communities. The game’s sanity-linked speed and curse are not historical diagnostic rules. [F15, P4]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • Freezing • Spirit Box | Forced: Spirit    50%                            ~1.5 at high sanity → ~2.25 at 0%; LOS can push    EXTREME at low sanity\nBox on 1–2 evidence                                                              ~3.7\nMoroi — dossier details\nCore mechanics               Its base hunt speed increases as average team sanity falls. At very low sanity it is one of the fastest possible ghosts once\nLOS acceleration is added.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  A successful Spirit Box response or relevant paranormal sound can curse the listener, causing substantially faster passive\nsanity drain even in lit areas. Sanity medication removes the curse. Incense blinds a Moroi for about 7 s rather than 5 s.\nBest controlled test        Track speed across controlled hunts at very different sanity levels. If a player received Spirit Box/paramic evidence and\nsanity plummets despite light discipline, consider the curse.\n0-evidence identification   Sanity-linked speed is the clean tell; do not confuse normal LOS acceleration with a true base-speed change.\nCommon confusion        Thaye changes with time/age, Deildegast with props, Obambo with timed states.\nSurvival note           Do not let team sanity collapse. Carry medication and incense; the longer blind duration is useful, but a 0%-sanity Moroi can\nerase distance rapidly.\n14. Myling\nPronunciation MEE-ling is a rough aid; Swedish y is an ee-like vowel spoken with rounded lips.\nCultural origin Sweden and wider Scandinavian dead-child traditions; well-attested.\nTraditional lore A myling is commonly the restless ghost of a secretly killed or improperly buried infant. Stories let the child reveal the\ncrime, call for recognition or seek a proper burial; details differ locally. These are tragic tales about concealed death and social\nobligations, not simply a tradition of unusually quiet adult ghosts. [F16, P5]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • EMF 5 • UV                      50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                          Sneaky approach\nduring hunts\nMyling — dossier details\nCore mechanics               Its hunt vocalizations and footsteps are audible from a shorter range than normal—about 12 m versus roughly 20 m for a\nstandard ghost.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Produces paranormal sounds more often, making sound-recording / parabolic work more active.\nBest controlled test         Classic flashlight-interference test: while safely hidden, compare when your held/near electronic interference begins versus\nwhen footsteps become audible. With a Myling, interference can begin before you hear it.\n0-evidence identification   Short hunt-audio range is the core behavior tell. Use repeated hunts because walls/floors/audio settings can muddy one\nsample.\nCommon confusion       A quiet ghost or obstructed audio can fake it.\nSurvival note           Do not rely on hearing alone to judge distance. Watch electronics/interference and use prepared hiding routes.\n15. Obake\nPronunciation oh-bah-keh; three Japanese syllables, final eh rather than ay.\nCultural origin Japan; well-attested broad word associated with transformation, apparitions and uncanny beings.\nTraditional lore Obake and bakemono are connected with changing or transforming. They can describe shapeshifting creatures and, in\neveryday use, ghosts or monsters more broadly. An obake is not invariably a dead human or a creature with extra fingers. The game’s\nchanging model draws on the transformation theme; its fingerprints are a game-specific clue. [F17, P6]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nEMF 5 • Orbs • UV | Forced: UV on 1–2 evidence  50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                           Standard speed;\nstrong visual tells\nObake — dossier details\nCore mechanics          Has a reduced chance to leave UV evidence on eligible interactions, and its UV marks fade faster. It can leave unique\nabnormal prints such as a six-finger handprint.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  During a hunt, it can briefly shapeshift into another ghost model for a blink. UV is guaranteed on reduced-evidence modes\nwith at least one evidence.\nBest controlled test       Check every eligible UV interaction carefully and learn the abnormal print patterns. During safe hunt observation, watch for\na one-blink model change.\n0-evidence identification   Hunt shapeshift is the conclusive 0-evidence test.\nCommon confusion        Missing UV once is not exclusion; Obake is specifically allowed to skip prints more often.\nSurvival note              Standard hunt survival. If hunt testing, view from a safe loop/hiding observation angle and prioritize survival over staring at\nthe model.\n16. Obambo\nPronunciation oh-BAHM-boh; tentative English reading, not a verified local-language pronunciation.\nCultural origin Equatorial west-central Africa, especially historical accounts from the Gabon region; attested name with a limited and\nmediated source record.\nTraditional lore Older accounts use Obambou for a spirit associated with possession or the unsettled dead. One tradition describes a\nwandering spirit asking relatives for a small dwelling near their home. Colonial writers often imposed words such as “devil” on local\nbeliefs, so these reports need context and should not be generalized to all African cultures. [F18, F19]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • UV • DOTS                       10% calm / 65% aggressive      1.445 calm / 1.955 aggressive + normal LOS        State-dependent;\naggressive state\ndangerous early\nObambo — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Always begins calm. One minute after the exit door is first opened it switches state, then flips every two minutes\n(community-documented timing). It can change state during a hunt. Calm has high ambient activity but a 10% hunt\nthreshold and slow base speed; aggressive has lower ambient activity but a 65% threshold and fast base speed.\nObambo — dossier details\nAbility / hidden mechanic  A hunt that begins while aggressive is 20% shorter, even if the ghost switches state mid-hunt.\nBest controlled test       Time activity phases and hunt speed. A sudden mid-hunt base-speed snap with no obvious LOS cause is strong. On a known\nmap/difficulty, time an aggressive-start hunt for the 20% shorter duration.\n0-evidence identification   Timed two-state cycle + opposite activity/aggression pattern is the signature.\nCommon confusion        Twins can produce two hunt base speeds; Gallu also has states; neither flips on Obambo’s timed cycle.\nSurvival note           Do not mistake a “quiet” aggressive phase for safety. At 65% it may hunt while barely interacting.\n17. Oni\nPronunciation oh-nee; two Japanese syllables.\nCultural origin Japan; well-attested, diverse ogre and demon traditions.\nTraditional lore Oni are often pictured as large horned beings carrying clubs and wearing tiger-skin clothing. Stories portray\nfrightening attackers or punishers, while some local traditions allow protective or more sympathetic roles. They are not all ghosts of\ndead humans. Their strength and imposing appearance are cultural motifs; the game’s visibility and event restrictions are separate. [F20]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • EMF 5 • Freezing                    50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                           Standard speed;\nconspicuous\nmanifestations\nOni — dossier details\nCore mechanics          More visible during hunts, spending a greater fraction of its blink cycle visible. It cannot perform the mist/airball ghost\nevent.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Oni ghost events are intense. As of v0.19.0.0, the intended sanity drain on collision during singing manifestations is 20%\n(the patch fixed a prior 10% bug).\nBest controlled test       Watch hunt blink visibility from safety and note event type history. A ghost that repeatedly manifests but never does an\nairball supports Oni; one missing event type is not proof.\n0-evidence identification   Very visible hunt blinking + impossible airball event are the core behavior set.\nCommon confusion       Phantom is the opposite blink extreme—much longer invisible periods.\nSurvival note              Standard survival. Treat frequent manifestations as sanity pressure even if it is not hunting yet.\n18. Onryo\nPronunciation ohn-ryoh; Japanese onryō, with a longer final oh. Keep ryo together.\nCultural origin Japan; well-attested vengeful-spirit tradition.\nTraditional lore An onryō is a spirit whose grievance or resentment brings harm after death. Historical accounts link such spirits with\ncalamity and efforts at ritual pacification; theater and later horror fiction develop more personal revenge narratives. Both male and\nfemale figures occur. The game’s fear of flames and candle-count rules do not define the traditional onryō. [F10, F21, P7]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nOrbs • Freezing • Spirit Box                  ~60% normally; flame            1.7 m/s + normal LOS                        Can force hunts\ninteractions modify hunt logic                                                through flame count\nOnryo — dossier details\nCore mechanics                Lit firelights near the ghost can block hunt attempts, taking priority over crucifixes. A blocked attempt extinguishes a flame.\nThe ghost also tracks extinguished flames; a repeated “third flame” pattern can trigger a hunt at any sanity.\nAbility / hidden mechanic   In practical testing, flames are both protection and a trigger. This creates the classic Onryo candle test: controlled flames\nnear crucifix coverage and careful counting.\nBest controlled test        Set multiple firelights near the ghost with crucifix backup. Track each extinguish and whether a hunt tries to fire on/after\nthe third counted flame. Reset your assumptions after complex cursed-item or room-change behavior.\n0-evidence identification   Consistent flame-block/hunt behavior is the main proof.\nCommon confusion       Any ghost can blow out candles; random timing can fake the “third candle” if you do not count carefully.\nSurvival note             Never run an Onryo test without crucifix coverage and an escape route. Flames are not unconditional safety.\n19. Phantom\nPronunciation FAN-tum.\nCultural origin European literary and ordinary-language term with Greek roots; broad apparition category.\nTraditional lore A phantom is an appearance or specter, often the seeming presence of someone absent or dead. Stories may make\nsuch an apparition a warning, a memory or a frightening visitor. The word does not specify one culture’s creature or a consistent\nbehavior pattern. Disappearing in a photograph is a Phasmophobia rule, not a dictionary definition. [F22]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • UV • Spirit Box                     50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                           Harder to track\nvisually in a hunt\nPhantom — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Spends longer invisible during hunt blink cycles, making it look like it “teleports” between visible flashes. Looking at a\nmanifested Phantom drains sanity faster.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  A successful ghost photo during a manifestation makes the Phantom disappear visually while the event can continue; the\nphoto can lack the visible ghost model.\nBest controlled test       Take a clean ghost photo during an event and watch for disappearance. During a hunt, compare its long invisible blink\nwindows against Oni/standard.\n0-evidence identification   Photo disappearance or characteristic blink pattern are strong.\nCommon confusion        Oni is unusually visible; bad lighting can make any ghost seem invisible.\nSurvival note           Do not stare longer than needed. Hunt by sound/route, not visual tracking alone.\n20. Poltergeist\nPronunciation POHL-ter-gyst; gyst rhymes with heist.\nCultural origin German term and wider European haunting traditions; well-attested name for a noisy spirit or disturbance.\nTraditional lore Accounts focus on knocks, thrown objects and seemingly unexplained physical disruption. The German components\nrefer to making a racket and to a spirit. Later psychical-research literature also debated explanations other than a dead person’s ghost.\nThe game’s object-throwing theme echoes these accounts, but precise probabilities and sanity effects are invented mechanics. [F3, F23]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • UV • Spirit Box                    50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                               Sanity damage from\nobject-heavy rooms\nPoltergeist — dossier details\nCore mechanics          Has an exceptionally high relationship with throwable objects and can throw multiple nearby props essentially at once.\nDuring hunts it throws environmental objects much more frequently than most ghosts.\nAbility / hidden mechanic   Its multi-object throw can drain sanity based on the number of affected objects.\nBest controlled test         Build a “polty pile” of many small throwables in the active room and watch from safety. A simultaneous explosive spread is\nthe classic confirmation.\n0-evidence identification   Multi-throw is highly distinctive when clearly observed.\nCommon confusion        High RNG activity can make any ghost seem throw-happy; one thrown cup proves nothing.\nSurvival note           Do not loiter at low sanity in a prop-rich room just to farm activity. Set the pile, back off, and observe.\n21. Raiju\nPronunciation rye-joo; Japanese raijū, with a longer final oo.\nCultural origin Japan; well-attested thunder-beast tradition.\nTraditional lore Raijū are beasts associated with thunderstorms and lightning. Historical depictions vary from small mammal-like\ncreatures to stranger composite forms; there is no single required animal shape. They give lightning an animal presence in story and\nimage. The game extends the storm association into modern electronic equipment, which is a thematic adaptation. [F24, P8]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • EMF 5 • Orbs                      50% normally / 65% near         1.7 base / ~2.5 near active electronics            EXTREME around an\nqualifying active electronics                                                     evidence station\nRaiju — dossier details\nCore mechanics             Active qualifying electronics near the ghost raise its hunt threshold and set a fast hunt speed around 2.5 m/s. Its electronic\ninterference radius is also larger than normal.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  The danger follows electronics in the environment, not just the item in your hand. An evidence station full of powered gear\ncan turn the favorite room into a speed zone.\nBest controlled test        During a controlled hunt, compare footsteps as it enters/leaves the radius of clearly active electronics. You should hear a\nsharp speed change.\n0-evidence identification    Electronics-linked speed zone + larger interference range is the signature.\nCommon confusion          Jinn is fast at range with breaker ON; Moroi/Thaye can simply be fast.\nSurvival note             Turn off/drop unnecessary active electronics before provoking hunts and do not hide beside powered equipment.\n22. Revenant\nPronunciation REV-uh-nunt; usual English reading.\nCultural origin European returning-dead traditions; English borrowed from French revenant, one who returns.\nTraditional lore Medieval and later stories describe dead people returning, sometimes bodily and sometimes as apparitions, to trouble\nthe living. Unresolved wrongdoing, death and burial can matter, but accounts differ. “Revenant” names the return rather than a fixed\nability set; the game’s slow-searching and fast-chasing phases are not a universal folklore pattern. [F3, F25]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • Orbs • Freezing                   50%                            ~1.0 searching → ~3.0 after detecting a player     EXTREME once\ndetected\nRevenant — dossier details\nCore mechanics            Very slow while it has no player detection waypoint, but rockets to about 3.0 m/s after detecting a player through sight,\nvoice or electronics.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  The signature is not just “fast ghost”—it is the enormous switch between slow searching and fast pursuit.\nBest controlled test         Listen from a safe, silent hiding spot. If footsteps are abnormally slow, then become extremely fast after a deliberate distant\ndetection and later slow again, Revenant is very likely.\n0-evidence identification    Slow-to-instant-fast transition is one of the cleanest 0-evidence tells.\nCommon confusion       Deogen starts fast toward hidden players then slows close—the opposite. Deildegast can be 3.0 from hunt start but remains\nconstant for that hunt.\nSurvival note             Never let it detect you unless you already have distance, incense and a route. Electronics discipline matters enormously.\n23. Shade\nPronunciation SHAYD.\nCultural origin English literary ghost term, used especially of the dead in classical-underworld writing and translation.\nTraditional lore A shade is the shadow-like presence or soul of a dead person. The term evokes an insubstantial continuation of life\nrather than one named monster. A shade may speak or retain memory in literary accounts; shyness, low activity and reluctance to hunt\nnear people are the game’s specialization of the word. [F26]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • EMF 5 • Freezing                  35%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                              Later hunter, but\nbehavior can be\nmisleading\nShade — dossier details\nCore mechanics            Extremely shy. Its interactions and events are strongly suppressed around players, and it cannot initiate a hunt while a\nplayer is in the same room as the ghost’s actual current location.\nAbility / hidden mechanic   Late natural hunt threshold (~35%) is one of the strongest statistical clues. However, room boundaries and ghost roaming\ncan make “same room” tests look inconsistent.\nBest controlled test        At low sanity, stay in the correctly identified current room while protected and observe whether hunt attempts only occur\nafter the ghost roams out. Use motion/EMF to understand location rather than favorite-room label alone.\n0-evidence identification   Late hunt + persistent player-proximity suppression is the behavior package.\nCommon confusion       Any ghost can be passive by RNG. “It did nothing for five minutes” is not a Shade test.\nSurvival note                   Its late threshold gives more setup time, but do not become complacent once team sanity crosses the mid-30s.\n24. Spirit\nPronunciation SPIR-it.\nCultural origin Broad cross-cultural concept; English word through Latin spiritus, breath or spirit.\nTraditional lore Spirit can mean a soul, an incorporeal being or a nonhuman supernatural presence depending on the religion and\ncontext. Stories of the dead lingering through attachment or unfinished obligations occur in many places, but should not be treated as\none universal doctrine. Phasmophobia gives this very broad label a specific evidence combination and incense timer. [F27]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nWriting • EMF 5 • Spirit Box                  50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                           Standard\nSpirit — dossier details\nCore mechanics          The baseline ghost with one major hidden mechanic: after a successful incense cleanse outside a hunt, it cannot start a\nregular hunt for about 180 s instead of the usual 90 s.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Otherwise behaves close to standard mechanics, so it is often identified by exclusion or the incense timer.\nBest controlled test        Cleanse the ghost correctly, start a timer from the actual successful cleanse, and maintain hunt-eligible sanity. If no hunt\noccurs for roughly three minutes, Spirit rises sharply in probability.\n0-evidence identification   Incense timer is the strongest behavior test.\nCommon confusion       Bad cleanse placement can produce a false timer. Demon is the short 60 s opposite.\nSurvival note              Standard survival. Use the long post-cleanse safety window to finish objectives rather than waiting in danger.\n25. Thaye\nPronunciation thuh-YEH; two syllables, th as in thin; Burmese tone is only approximated.\nCultural origin Myanmar; established Burmese word for ghost, with varied local and Buddhist-influenced traditions.\nTraditional lore Thayé is not one uniquely named individual. Accounts of these ghosts describe frightening or troublesome dead,\nsometimes linked to illness and unsettled relationships with the living. Appearances and explanations vary, and the word should not be\nequated with the entire nat tradition. Rapidly aging when investigators remain nearby is the game’s invention. [F28, P9]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • Writing • Orbs                      ~75% young → ~15% old         ~2.75 young → ~1.0 old; fixed by age            EXTREME early; weak\nlate\nThaye — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Ages when players spend time near it. With age it becomes slower, less active, and hunts at lower and lower sanity. It starts\namong the fastest and earliest hunters and can end as one of the slowest/least aggressive.\nAbility / hidden mechanic   Its age can be queried with the Ouija Board; repeated age answers can confirm progression. It does not gain normal LOS\nacceleration because its hunt speed is age-defined.\nBest controlled test       Compare an early hunt to a much later hunt after spending time near the ghost. A large, progressive slowdown is classic\nThaye.\n0-evidence identification   Time/age-linked progressive slowdown is the signature.\nCommon confusion      Obambo flips states back and forth; Deildegast resets per hunt and responds to props. Thaye trends one direction over the\ncontract.\nSurvival note               Treat a fresh Thaye like an emergency. Get protection down early; if you survive long enough near it, later hunts become\neasier.\n26. The Mimic\nPronunciation thuh MIM-ik.\nCultural origin English descriptive label used as a game type; imitation is a widespread story motif.\nTraditional lore Doubles, deceivers and shapeshifters appear in many traditions, but that does not establish a single historical ghost\nspecies called “The Mimic.” The game makes imitation into a distinct mechanical identity. Its false Orbs and changing copied behaviors\nare part of that design; a connection to any particular folklore figure needs separate evidence. [F29, G2]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nUV • Freezing • Spirit Box + fake Orbs | Forced:    Copies current ghost behavior   Copies current ghost behavior                 UNPREDICTABLE\nFake Orbs always, even 0 evidence\nThe Mimic — dossier details\nCore mechanics          Can imitate the behavior, hunt threshold, speed and abilities of other ghosts, changing mimic target periodically. It does not\nchange type mid-hunt. Ghost Orbs are an extra ability, not one of its three standard evidences, and appear even on 0-\nevidence settings.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  Because it copies behavior, a Mimic can produce “impossible” combinations across a contract: e.g., one hunt looks like a\nDeogen, a later hunt like a Hantu. It also retains certain internal counters/ongoing effects in ways that can make advanced\ntests messy.\nBest controlled test       Whenever Orbs are present, keep Mimic open until UV + Freezing + Spirit Box have been checked according to difficulty. On\n0 evidence, Orbs themselves are the giveaway because normal evidence is disabled but Mimic Orbs remain.\n0-evidence identification   Ghost Orbs on true 0-evidence = Mimic. In reduced evidence, contradictory changing behavior plus Orbs is suspicious.\nCommon confusion        Every ghost—it is designed to impersonate them.\nSurvival note             Never rely on one hunt’s behavior as a permanent safety rule. A Mimic can change into a much more dangerous ghost\nbefore the next hunt.\n27. The Twins\nPronunciation thuh TWINZ.\nCultural origin English descriptive label used as a game type; no single established folklore origin is specified.\nTraditional lore Twin figures can be divine, heroic, ominous or ordinary across different traditions. A generic motif is not evidence that\nthis ghost derives from a particular pair of mythological twins or from a film’s imagery. Phasmophobia’s two-range interactions belong to\nits fictional type; they do not demonstrate two separate historical spirits. [F30, G2]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nEMF 5 • Freezing • Spirit Box                 50%                           ~1.53 slow hunt / ~1.87 fast hunt + normal LOS     Unpredictable hunt\norigin and pace\nThe Twins — dossier details\nCore mechanics            Mechanically one ghost with two interaction ranges / “twin” behavior. It can create interactions far apart in a short window\nand hunts can originate from different effective locations. Hunt base speed is chosen from a slower or faster twin value.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  The speed alternation is moderate—not the extreme Revenant/Deogen type. Activity-monitor “twinteraction” patterns are\nsupporting, not conclusive.\nBest controlled test       Compare several hunts. If one is consistently a little slow and another a little fast at base before LOS buildup, Twins is\nplausible. Track widely separated interaction timing.\n0-evidence identification   Alternating mild hunt speeds across multiple hunts is the strongest behavior clue.\nCommon confusion       Aswang starts around slow-Twin pace; Obambo uses similar two speeds but on a timed state system and can change mid-\nhunt.\nSurvival note           Do not assume the ghost will hunt from the favorite room. Keep routes safe from adjacent/remote interaction areas.\n28. Wraith\nPronunciation RAYTH; silent w, th as in thin.\nCultural origin Scots and Scottish literary usage; well-attested apparition term, with uncertain deeper etymology.\nTraditional lore A wraith can be the likeness of a living person seen as a death omen, or an apparition of someone dead. Later fantasy\noften makes wraiths uniformly sinister, but that is not the whole history of the word. Floating, teleporting and avoiding salt are the\ngame’s particular interpretation. [F3, F31]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • EMF 5 • Spirit Box                   50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                              Teleport ability can\nplace it near players\nWraith — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Never disturbs salt. It can teleport near a player outside hunts, creating an EMF reading near the destination and potentially\ncausing sudden activity away from the favorite room.\nWraith — dossier details\nAbility / hidden mechanic   Salt immunity is a physical mechanic, not a personality tendency. However, an enraged Gallu can also fail to disturb salt, so\ncontext matters in the expanded ghost pool.\nBest controlled test        Place salt directly in likely ghost pathing, ideally use motion evidence to confirm it walked through. If it repeatedly crosses\nwithout disturbing salt from a neutral state, Wraith is a top suspect.\n0-evidence identification    Verified salt pass-through is the classic test.\nCommon confusion        Enraged Gallu can ignore salt temporarily; be aware of recent crucifix/incense/salt state changes.\nSurvival note           Do not assume distance from the favorite room means safety; teleport activity can move the ghost close before a hunt.\n29. Yokai\nPronunciation yoh-kye; Japanese yōkai, with a longer first oh.\nCultural origin Japan; well-attested umbrella category of uncanny beings and phenomena.\nTraditional lore Yōkai can include shapeshifting animals, animated objects, oni and many other strange presences. The category has\nchanged over time and is not simply a synonym for one species of human ghost. Traditions can be comic, unsettling or dangerous. The\ngame’s sensitivity to nearby speech and poor hunt detection do not describe yōkai as a whole. [F17, F20, P10]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • Orbs • Spirit Box                    50% normally; up to ~80% when  1.7 m/s + normal LOS                                Early hunt if team\ntalking very near it                                                                         talks in its face\nYokai — dossier details\nCore mechanics           Nearby player voice can raise its hunt threshold dramatically. During hunts it has very poor detection range for voice and\nactive handheld electronics—roughly 2.5 m instead of the much larger normal range. Visual detection remains normal.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  The famous “deaf ghost” test only works if you are out of sight; if it can see you, it will chase normally.\nBest controlled test       From a safe hidden position outside visual LOS, use a controlled active electronic/voice bait at a distance that a normal\nghost should detect. If it repeatedly ignores the signal until very close, Yokai is likely.\n0-evidence identification   Short voice/electronics detection is the best tell.\nCommon confusion       Kormos has special footstep hearing but near-blind vision; Yokai’s weakness is voice/electronics, not footsteps.\nSurvival note            Once hidden and out of sight, stay quiet/off anyway. Do not talk near the ghost at high sanity unless you are intentionally\ntesting.\n30. Yurei\nPronunciation yoo-ray; Japanese yūrei, with a longer first oo.\nCultural origin Japan; well-attested term for spirits of the dead, with changing historical meanings.\nTraditional lore Unresolved attachments and emotions often keep a yūrei connected to the living in stories. The familiar white burial\nclothing, loose hair and absent feet are conventions of particular artistic and theatrical traditions, not a universal appearance. Vengeful\nspirits overlap with this category. Door-slamming and measured sanity loss remain game rules. [F21, P11]\nGameplay Evidence, tests and survival rules below describe Phasmophobia.\nEvidence                      Hunt threshold                      Hunt speed                      Danger\nDOTS • Orbs • Freezing                    50%                              1.7 m/s + normal LOS                               Sanity drain and door\nability\nYurei — dossier details\nCore mechanics          Can use a door-closing ability that fully closes a nearby open door and drains about 15% sanity from players near the ghost.\nIt is one of the few ways the front/exit door can be manipulated outside a hunt/event context.\nAbility / hidden mechanic  A successful incense cleanse also forces a Yurei back to its favorite room and prevents it from roaming out for about 90 s,\nthough events can still occur elsewhere.\nBest controlled test       Watch for a distinct full door slam/close paired with sanity loss and no event. After cleansing, use motion/room activity to\ntest temporary confinement.\n0-evidence identification   Door ability is the strongest tell, but it is RNG-gated—lack of a slam is not exclusion.\nCommon confusion       Normal ghosts touch doors constantly; only the specific full-close + sanity-drain behavior is meaningful.\nSurvival note              Standard survival. Use the post-incense confinement to stabilize evidence equipment and complete room-based tests.","status":"baseline"},{"id":5,"title":"Hunt-Identification Indexes","page":18,"lastPage":18,"text":"Speed families\nWhat you hear                             Think first                       How to separate\nVery slow until detection, then ~3.0                      Revenant                        Detection-triggered switch.\n~3.0 far, crawls close                               Deogen                       Always knows player location; never hide.\nStarts up to 3.0, constant within hunt; prop-controlled       Deildegast                 Move unique map props before next attempt.\nbetween hunts\n2.75-ish early, trends slower over contract                Thaye                       Ages one direction with player proximity.\nSpeed follows room temperature                       Hantu                   No normal LOS accel; cold = faster.\nSpeed follows average sanity                           Moroi                       Lower team sanity = higher base speed.\nFast only near active electronics                              Raiju                         Sharp speed zone around electronics.\nFast at distance with breaker ON + LOS                        Jinn                         Drops out when close/breaker off.\n1.2 or 2.25 based on nearby player still/moving           Dayan                        Movement-linked within 10 m.\nTwo mild base speeds across hunts                       Twins                          ~1.53 vs ~1.87; remote interactions.\nTimed calm/aggressive speeds                     Obambo                       1.445 vs 1.955; state flips every ~2 min.\nProtection-linked slow/normal/fast states                    Gallu                           1.36 / 1.7 / 1.955; state equipment interaction.\n1.53 base but unusually fast LOS buildup                Aswang                      Reaches ~2.53 max faster than standard.\nFast toward heard footsteps / near-blind                Kormos                       Stop moving; footstep ranges are distinctive.\nBlink / visibility families\nObservation                                              Ghost / interpretation\nLong invisible gaps during hunt                              Phantom.\nUnusually visible / short invisible gaps                              Oni.\nOne-blink model change                                  Obake shapeshift.\nVisible ghost breath with breaker off                          Hantu (during hunt).\nAny ghost looks “invisible” because lighting is bad             Do not call Phantom without repeated blink timing or photo test.\nSound / detection families\nObservation                                                        Think\nHunt footsteps/vocals only audible unusually close                       Myling.\nVoice/electronics bait ignored until very close while out of LOS             Yokai.\nFootsteps cause long-range pursuit; still player can be nearly invisible    Kormos.\nUnique scream in paranormal audio                                  Banshee.\nSpirit Box / paranormal sound followed by abnormal sanity drain        Moroi curse.\nObject / door / light families\nObservation                                                        Think\nMulti-object explosive throw                                                        Poltergeist.\nInstantly switches a just-turned-on room light back off                        Mare.\nFull door-close ability + nearby sanity drain                                         Yurei.\nNever disturbs salt                                                         Wraith — unless Gallu state context explains it.\nSalt works once, then ghost ignores later piles after protection/state shift         Gallu.\nCamera-only DOTS with room empty                                         Goryo.\nRepeated flame extinguishes / hunt blocks tied to firelight count                Onryo.","status":"baseline"},{"id":6,"title":"Equipment & Tier Mechanics","page":18,"lastPage":19,"text":"TIER PRINCIPLE: Higher tier is not always “better” for every test. Tier II UV is non-electronic and can be hunt-safe light; Tier I incense\nlets a blinded fast ghost keep moving away rather than freezing near you; Tier I thermometer is passive/non-electronic.\nEvidence equipment\nTool                        Tier I                         Tier II                         Tier III                                Field notes\nEMF Reader               Basic short-range analog      Improved digital             Longer-range / directional     True EMF 5 is evidence; hunt interference can\ndisplay                   mask readings.\nDOTS                    $65; 5 m narrow spotlight      $65; 2.5 m area                $65; 7 m wide scanning        Place for ghost pathing, not just favorite-room\ncenter. Goryo camera rule.\nUV Light                  $35; narrow electronic; 5 s     $35; glowstick, 60 s light, 10 s  $35; wide electronic; 1.5 s      Glowstick is excellent hunt-safe illumination.\ncharge                       charge; non-electronic        charge\nThermometer             $30; passive, 0.33°C/s; non-    $30; 2.5 s sample; electronic    $30; 1.5 s sample; higher       Freezing is ≤0°C / 32°F; visible breath is not\nelectronic                                                 accuracy                       proof.\nVideo Camera            $50; low image/resistance      $50; medium                  $50; high                    Night vision for Orbs; higher tiers resist\nparanormal interference better.\nGhost Writing Book      3 m range; ~51% success per  4 m; ~67%                  5 m; ~76%                              If Writing is available, ghost attempts periodically\nwriting attempt                                                                          (about 45–90 s).\nSpirit Box              3 m response range          4 m                       5 m                    Same room as ghost + lights off; text mode is\nuseful when voice recognition is unreliable.\nProtection / support equipment\nTool                                      Tier mechanics                                               Expert use\nCrucifix                    T1: 3 m / 1 use. T2: 4 m / 2 uses. T3: 5 m / 2 uses; unused T3 can stop  Works through walls/doors but same-floor geometry matters.\none cursed hunt by consuming both uses.                    Demon +50% range. Gallu changes range by state.\nIncense                T1 range 3 m, burn 5 s. T2 4 m/6 s + slows ghost to 50% during 5 s     Regular hunt-prevention timer: Demon 60 s, Spirit 180 s,\nblind. T3 5 m/7 s + halts ghost during initial 5 s blind.                 others 90 s. Hunt blind: most 5 s; Gallu enraged 4 / weakened\n6; Moroi 7.\nSalt                         Tiers increase uses/effects; T3 can slow a hunting ghost temporarily.   Wraith never disturbs it; enraged Gallu also ignores it. Use\npath confirmation before drawing conclusions.\nFirelight                 Higher tiers last longer / reduce passive sanity drain more strongly;   Core Onryo test/protection tool. Lighting the ghost room can\nT3 is indefinite and weatherproof.                                 conserve sanity while gathering evidence.\nSanity Medication         Preset restore: Amateur 40%, Intermediate 35%, Professional 30%,    Moroi curse is removed by medication. Coordinate team\nNightmare 25%, Insanity 20%; higher tiers restore faster; T3 adds     doses instead of wasting pills at high sanity.\ntemporary movement/stamina boost.\nParabolic Microphone     Higher tiers increase reach/features; T3 has advanced              Banshee scream; Myling produces paranormal sounds more\ndirectional/sonar-style behavior.                                        often; Moroi can curse via paranormal sound.\nMotion Sensor           Higher tiers expand shape/coverage; T3 can scan a broader area.     Confirm ghost path through salt, track room roaming,\nestablish where a Shade actually is.\nPhoto Camera           T1 3 s cooldown; T2 2 s; T3 1 s. Only first 5 photos count in the       Phantom photo test; capture bone, cursed item, interactions,\njournal.                                                                          crucifix, writing, UV, etc. v0.19 marks duplicate evidence.\nSound Recorder          Records unique paranormal sounds; higher tiers improve visual       Useful for Banshee/Myling/Moroi mechanics and Chronicle\nfeedback/range.                                            media rewards.\nHead Gear              T1 head camera; T2 head-mounted LED; T3 night-vision goggles.      T1 can let truck player scan Orbs; T3 vision helps dark\nnavigation but remains electronic.\nTripod                        Stability and motorized features improve by tier.                      Place video at eye-height/clean angle; ghosts can knock\ncameras over.\nSound Sensor          Remote area audio to truck monitor.                               Released baseline: remote area audio on the truck monitor\nfor triangulation. The announced Resonance map/UI rework\nis future content; see section 19.","status":"baseline"},{"id":7,"title":"Cursed Possessions","page":19,"lastPage":20,"text":"UNIVERSAL CURSED RULE: Using a cursed possession immediately cancels setup protection. A cursed hunt has 1 s grace and makes\nfuture hunts in that contract 20 s longer. Do not touch a cursed item until you know an escape route unless you are intentionally\ngambling.\nItem                             Exact value / trigger                                Best use                       Main danger\nHaunted Mirror          Shows current favorite room. Sanity drains ~7.5%/s, with a     Instant room location on large maps.    Staring too long can zero sanity and\nminimum cost per use; can break and curse-hunt at critically                                             trigger a cursed hunt in your hands.\nlow sanity.\nOuija Board              Question costs vary. Room/location is expensive (~50%);       Find room/bone, age a Thaye, gather   Board can break and cursed-hunt if\nmany basic questions ~5%; bone location ~20%. Say           media.                           abandoned while active, used without\n“Goodbye” before leaving it.                                                            enough sanity, or during Hide and\nSeek.\nItem                             Exact value / trigger                                Best use                       Main danger\nMusic Box               Ghost sings within range; close proximity can manifest/walk   Locate ghost and force a visible event.   Getting too close, dropping/throwing it\ntoward the box.                                                                                    while active, prolonged walk or\nreaching 0 sanity can trigger cursed\nhunt.\nSummoning Circle        5 candles, each costs 16% sanity; completing circle summons  Guaranteed ghost media / objectives    Very expensive sanity cost; if you\nghost for ~5 s then cursed hunt.                                 after identification.                   cannot pay, behavior can collapse into\nimmediate danger.\nTarot Cards              10 cards, random effects.                                  Powerful event/revive/sanity           Death = cursed hunt; Hanged Man =\nmanipulation and media.               death; probabilities mean there is no\n“safe” draw.\nVoodoo Doll             Normal pins force interactions for sanity cost; heart pin        Force doors/throws to gather           Heart pin can fire unexpectedly\ntriggers cursed hunt.                                      EMF/UV/writing opportunities.         depending on pin order/sanity.\nMonkey Paw             3–5 wishes depending on difficulty multiplier; each wish once.  Targeted rule-breaking: knowledge,     Every wish has a penalty; several\nrevive, leave, weather, activity, etc.       directly force hunts or permanent\ncontract debuffs.\nTarot Cards: probabilities & effects\nCard                    Approx. chance                                           Effect\nThe Tower                      20%                      Strongly increases potential ghost activity for ~20 s.\nWheel of Fortune                 20%                    Green/red outcome: +25 or −25 sanity.\nThe Fool                       17% normally; replaces    Appears as another card then becomes Fool; no effect.\ndraws during hunts\nThe Devil                       10%                      Triggers a ghost event near the nearest player.\nDeath                         10%                        Starts a cursed hunt.\nThe Hermit                     10%                      Forces ghost to favorite room / suppresses roaming for about 1 min.\nThe Sun                      5%                        Sets player sanity to 100%.\nThe Moon                     5%                        Sets player sanity to 0%.\nThe High Priestess               2%                       Revives the first dead player or reserves a future revive if nobody is dead.\nThe Hanged Man                1%                               Kills the drawer immediately.\nMonkey Paw wishes: practical consequence sheet\nWish                                         Benefit                                        Cost / danger\nI wish to see the ghost                      Forces a summoning-style manifestation at ghost        Exits lock; after ~5 s a cursed hunt begins; wisher suffers\nlocation.                                         darkened vision.\nI wish for activity                            Greatly increases activity for ~2 min.                  Fuse box breaks permanently and exits lock for ~2 min.\nI wish to trap the ghost                     Cleanses / traps ghost in favorite room about 1 min.    Nearby doors lock; after trap ends a hunt follows.\nI wish for sanity                             Sets all players to ~50% sanity.                         Passive sanity drain is increased for rest of contract and\nfavorite room can change (even affecting Goryo’s normal\nlimitation).\nI wish to be safe                       Opens nearest blocked hiding spot.                     Lights in current room shatter; ghost gains exceptional\nability to sense the wisher’s active electronics for the rest\nof contract.\nI wish to leave                            Unlocks exit during a hunt.                          Wisher is slowed and vision reduced briefly.\nI wish for life                               Revives first dead player.                       50% chance the wisher dies in exchange.\nI wish for knowledge                        Eliminates an incorrect evidence option / associated    Ghost teleports near wisher and cursed hunts;\nghost possibilities.                               permanent dark/muffled perception penalty.\nI wish for [weather]                     Changes weather.                                Temporary blindness and sanity cost.\nI wish for anything                    Random unused wish.                            You surrender control of the downside.","status":"baseline"},{"id":8,"title":"Difficulty, Custom & Apocalypse","page":20,"lastPage":21,"text":"Difficulty           Unlock      Reward        Start sanity      Evidence       Setup        Grace               Key restrictions\nAmateur                Base         ×1          100%             3             5:00          5 s          Most forgiving; 50% insurance.\nIntermediate              Lv 10        ×2          100%             3             2:30          4 s         25% insurance; fewer safety margins.\nProfessional               Lv 20        ×3          100%             3          None         3 s         No insurance; full standard evidence.\nNightmare                Lv 30        ×4          100%             2          None         2 s         One evidence hidden; sanity/activity\nmonitors disabled; fewer hiding places; kills\ncan extend hunts.\nDifficulty           Unlock      Reward        Start sanity      Evidence       Setup        Grace               Key restrictions\nInsanity                   Lv 40        ×6          75%              1          None         2 s         Two evidences hidden; severe\nhiding/monitor restrictions.\nCustom                   Lv 50         ×0–×15         0/25/50/75/100     0–3            Variable       0–5 s         Almost every mechanic tunable; v0.19 can\nforce ghost type, setting multiplier to 0.\nAPOCALYPSE: The Apocalypse challenge uses custom extreme conditions on full Sunny Meadows. Reward tiers are associated with\nincreasingly severe settings; the core skill is 0-evidence behavior identification under high ghost speed / low player safety. Practice on\n2 evidence → 1 evidence → 0 evidence instead of jumping straight from Professional.","status":"baseline"},{"id":9,"title":"Media, Rewards & Perfect Investigations","page":21,"lastPage":21,"text":"Chronicle-style media rewards distinguish Unique from Duplicate evidence. v0.19 adds duplicate indicators directly to Photo/Video/Sound\nequipment, reducing guesswork.\nThe first 5 journal photos count for photo rewards. Strong targets include Ghost, Bone, Cursed Possession, Ghost Writing, UV, used Crucifix,\ndisturbed Salt and clear interactions.\nVideo and Sound now matter as their own media systems; capture actual paranormal/evidence moments rather than filling the journal with\nrepeats.\nA Perfect Investigation is a contract-completion package, not just “correct ghost”: correct identification, objectives/bone/media requirements\nand high-quality unique coverage all matter under the current reward system.\nDifficulty multiplier matters dramatically. Dying can erase efficiency even when your ghost guess was correct, so do not farm one more\noptional interaction after your information is already sufficient.\nReward component                                                         Practical note\nCorrect ghost                           Core payout; large maps award more base XP for identification than small maps.\nOptional objectives                         Usually small individual payouts; complete only when risk is sensible.\nBone                                   Always worth finding when practical; map size affects reward.\nUnique media                         Use higher-tier cameras/recorders for better value and avoid duplicates.\nPerfect Investigation bonus                Requires a complete, high-quality contract rather than a single good photo.\nBlood Moon / event modifiers              Seasonal or special modifiers can change reward calculations.","status":"baseline"},{"id":10,"title":"Maps & Restricted Variants","page":21,"lastPage":22,"text":"MAP COUNT: The game’s base-location list is commonly described as 14 maps, with Restricted layouts treated as variants. v0.19 adds\nRestricted Prison, Brownstone High School and Point Hope in addition to Sunny Meadows Restricted.\nMap                    Class        Floors    Rooms*      Exits*                             High-value field note\n6 Tanglewood Drive                 Small        2          12         1          Compact, forgiving; strong loops around dining/train-table/car-style furniture;\nexcellent test map.\n42 Edgefield Road                   Small        3          16         1           Current released layout: dense/vertical; know closets before hunt range. A\nrework is announced for later 2026, not yet incorporated.\n10 Ridgeview Court                  Small        3          16         1          Dining/basement loop options; multiple floors can complicate audio.\n13 Willow Street                     Small        2          10         1        No classic closets/lockers; organic furniture hides; reworked in July 2026.\nNell's Diner                         Small        1          14         2           Cooler Room can sit around 1°C with breaker on—temperature trap; open\nkitchen/dining loops.\nGrafton Farmhouse                  Small        3          14         1         Reworked layout; multiple furniture loops/hiding zones.\nCamp Woodwind                    Small        1          11         1         Open sight lines, weather exposure, limited walls; learn tent/furniture route\ndiscipline.\nPoint Hope                    Medium      10         16         1             Vertical lighthouse; long travel drains sanity; stairs/pillars break LOS. Restricted\nvariant is Small.\nBleasdale Farmhouse             Medium      3          21         2         Reworked farmhouse; larger than it feels, attic/vertical audio matters.\nSunny Meadows Restricted         Medium       Var.        Subset     1        Random restricted wing; smaller than full asylum but still complex.\nPrison                        Medium      2          Large       Multiple    Long corridors/cell blocks; v0.19 adds a Small Restricted version.\nMaple Lodge Campsite            Medium      3          28         1          Large outdoor campground + cabin; generator travel and weather matter.\nBrownstone High School             Large        2          64         5           Very long halls; v0.19 Restricted versions are Medium.\nSunny Meadows Mental Institution    Large        2          69         1           Five-wing megamap; full version used for Apocalypse challenge.\n*Room/exit counts follow current wiki conventions where available. Restricted variants change playable areas and are not simply “same map but shorter.”\nMap discipline that scales to every location\nBefore evidence: locate fuse box and a nearby safe zone. On large maps, cursed possessions can save enormous search time if you accept the\nrisk.\nMark “official” hiding versus unofficial furniture gaps. Aswang’s special weakness only recognizes official hiding places enabled by the hiding-\nplace system.\nLooping is not equal on every map. A broad table that works on Tanglewood may be useless on Edgefield or a campsite because LOS cannot\nbe broken cleanly.\nOutdoor maps make temperature and weather interpretation harder. Use a thermometer, not intuition.\nOn multi-floor maps, audio above/below can mislead room location. T3 parabolic / directional tools and deliberate floor-by-floor sweeps are\nvaluable.\nFor Brownstone/Prison/Sunny Meadows, remote sensors and team role division save more sanity than repeatedly walking the whole building.","status":"baseline"},{"id":11,"title":"Solo / Team Workflows","page":22,"lastPage":22,"text":"Solo: efficient three-trip setup\nTrip                        Carry                                                     Goal\n1          Thermometer + EMF/Video + Flashlight/UV        Locate breaker, hiding, active area, possible Orbs.\n2         DOTS + Writing + complementary evidence        Build evidence station and reposition camera.\n3             Crucifix + protection / media tool                Secure hunt attempts, then collect objectives and targeted behavior tests.\nTeam roles that reduce chaos\nRole                                                                Job\nRoom hunter              Temp + EMF + map sweep; calls exact room/adjacent interactions.\nEvidence builder              DOTS/Writing/Video/UV placement and repositions when ghost roams.\nSafety lead                     Finds hiding/loops, places crucifix, tracks sanity/hunt timer and carries incense.\nMedia / truck analyst          Monitors Orbs/DOTS/cameras/sensors, logs duplicate media, tracks evidence selections. v0.19 journal shows\nteammate selections.\nCommunity-tested habits worth keeping\n“Two escape routes” beats memorizing one hiding spot. Hunt pathing, a roam, or a blocked door can invalidate Plan A.\nLight/noise/electronics discipline solves more deaths than fancy ghost knowledge: turn electronics off, mute, break LOS, then hide.\nDo not force 100% completion when the contract is already solved. Community advice consistently favors leaving alive with a strong answer\nover dying while farming one more optional.\nLearn zero evidence gradually: master Professional first, then Nightmare (2), Insanity (1), then true 0. Weekly challenges are good low-cost\npractice.\nWhen evidence seems “impossible,” first question the room. Multiple players independently checking for Orbs/Box/temps often catches a bad\nroom assumption.\nCommunity cheat sheets are excellent for live play, but mechanics change. Use them as a fast interface, then verify surprising claims against\ncurrent patch/wiki behavior.","status":"baseline"},{"id":12,"title":"Zero / Low-Evidence Decision System","page":22,"lastPage":23,"text":"THE ORDER MATTERS: In 0 evidence, start with passive hunt observations that do not require standing next to the ghost. Only move\ninto active bait tests after speed, audio, blink and sanity have eliminated safer candidates.\n1. Is there a Ghost Orb on true 0-evidence? → THE MIMIC.\n2. Does it sprint to every hiding player then crawl when close? → DEOGEN.\n3. Is it very slow until it detects someone, then ~3.0? → REVENANT.\n4. Does speed track room temperature with no normal LOS acceleration? → HANTU.\n5. Did a fresh hunt start near 3.0 and remain fixed, then become slower after moving unique map props? → DEILDEGAST.\n6. Does it progressively slow over the contract rather than reset? → THAYE.\n7. Does it speed sharply around active electronics? → RAIJU. Breaker-on range burst? → JINN.\n8. Does nearby player movement toggle 1.2/2.25? → DAYAN. Do footsteps drive a near-blind ghost? → KORMOS.\n9. Do hunts alternate mild slow/fast values? → TWINS. Timed two-state + activity inversion? → OBAMBO. Protection-linked 3 states? →\nGALLU.\n10. Does a slightly slow base accelerate unusually rapidly in LOS? → ASWANG.\n11. Normal-speed group: test blink (PHANTOM/ONI/OBAKE), sound (MYLING/YOKAI/BANSHEE), salt (WRAITH/GALLU),\nlight/door/object/flame behavior (MARE/YUREI/POLTERGEIST/ONRYO), incense timers (SPIRIT/DEMON), room confinement\n(GORYO/YUREI), and late/early thresholds.\nLow-evidence rule-out logic\nOn Nightmare (2 evidence), one of a ghost’s normal three is hidden unless a forced-evidence rule applies.\nOn Insanity (1 evidence), two are hidden; behavioral testing is not optional if several ghosts share the one clue.\nOn custom 0 evidence, standard evidence tools can still help locate/interact with the ghost, but “negative evidence” means nothing. Mimic\nOrbs remain.\nIf a forced evidence should be present and you repeatedly fail under correct conditions, reassess ghost room / equipment placement before\nruling the ghost out.\nNever use activity personality as a hard exclusion. Even a Shade can have active windows; even a Poltergeist can spend time doing almost\nnothing.","status":"baseline"},{"id":13,"title":"Current Bugs & “Do Not Rule Out”","page":23,"lastPage":23,"text":"Trap                                                           Correct interpretation\nVisible breath                         Not Freezing evidence. Confirm ≤0°C / 32°F with thermometer.\nNo UV on one touch                   Does not exclude UV; Obake is especially capable of skipping prints.\nBook thrown blank                      Only excludes Writing on a mode where Writing is guaranteed available as a normal evidence option.\nNo Spirit Box response                 You may be outside same room/range, lights may be on, response RNG may fail, or Box may be hidden by difficulty.\nActivity monitor spike                  Not direct EMF 5 proof.\nNo Orbs in first camera angle            Ghost room may be wrong, Orbs may be occluded, favorite room may have moved. Sweep multiple angles.\nSalt untouched                          Wraith is likely only if you verified the ghost crossed it; enraged Gallu can also ignore salt.\n“Fast ghost”                              At least a dozen ghosts can be fast for different reasons. Identify the condition that makes it fast.\n“Quiet ghost”                        RNG. Obambo aggressive is intentionally low activity; Shade is shy; many normal ghosts can simply roll quiet.\nHunt started early                      Could be ghost threshold, ability, cursed possession, custom setting, average-sanity math or a player-specific\nBanshee condition.\nGallu salt visuals in multiplayer              Earlier community documentation reports a non-host visual bug after incense in some states. Persistence in this\nbuild is not confirmed; prefer host observation and state-speed confirmation.\nAswang “safe spot”                      Only official enabled hiding places trigger its no-kill hunt ending. Unofficial corners do not.\nOni event sanity values                    v0.19 fixed singing-event collision to intended 20%; old sheets that say 10% are stale.\nOld “new ghost” evidence sheets           Several third-party pages have carried outdated/incorrect evidence. Current evidence table + current patch notes\nwin.\nTanglewood exterior glow                    Official v0.19.1.0 known issue on Xbox/Steam: outside lampposts can glow incorrectly. This is not ghost evidence.\nCustom fuse-box visibility                     Official v0.19.1.0 known issue: the truck map can reveal the fuse-box location when Custom settings should hide it.\nOld Kormos wall-kill claims                Fixed in v0.18.0.0. Sheets describing kills through walls or beyond intended distance are stale.","status":"baseline"},{"id":14,"title":"Dense Quick-Reference Cards","page":23,"lastPage":24,"text":"If this happens, think…\nObservation                                                        Think\nOrbs on true 0-evidence                                                               MIMIC\nOne-blink model change                                                            OBAKE\nPhoto makes ghost vanish                                                     PHANTOM\nVery visible hunt; never airball                                                          ONI\nVerified salt pass without disturbance                                                   WRAITH / check GALLU state\nAudio heard only very close                                                           MYLING\nVoice/electronics ignored out of LOS until close                                                YOKAI\nUnique paranormal scream                                                           BANSHEE\nMulti-object explosion                                                                     POLTERGEIST\nInstant light switch-off                                                           MARE\nCamera-only DOTS, empty room                                                   GORYO\nDoor slam ability + sanity hit                                                                  YUREI\nFlame count controls hunts                                                      ONRYO\nFast in cold / no LOS accel                                                        HANTU\nBreaker-on range burst                                                                          JINN\nElectronics speed zone                                                                             RAIJU\nObservation                                                        Think\nFast far / crawl close                                                            DEOGEN\nSlow search / 3.0 detected                                                             REVENANT\nProgressively ages/slows                                                               THAYE\nFaster as sanity falls                                                            MOROI\nMovement near toggles 1.2/2.25                                                     DAYAN\nFootsteps drive blind pursuit                                                    KORMOS\nMap props slow next hunt                                                                 DEILDEGAST\nRapid LOS accel from ~1.53                                                     ASWANG\nProtection creates 3 states                                                            GALLU\nTimed 10%/65% state cycle                                                   OBAMBO\nIncense blocks hunts ~180 s                                                                          SPIRIT\nIncense only ~60 s / 20 s cooldown                                              DEMON\nAlternating mild hunt speeds                                                           TWINS\nVery shy; ~35% threshold                                                            SHADE\nHunt survival card\nDo                                                 Do NOT\nPre-check hides + two routes.                                   Assume favorite room is where hunt will start.\nTurn handheld electronics OFF before hiding.                            Carry a powered camera/flashlight into the closet.\nBreak LOS before entering hide.                                          Let the ghost watch you enter.\nSave sprint for sight breaks / route transitions.                             Sprint continuously and arrive exhausted.\nCarry incense for deliberate tests.                                        Test a fast ghost with no protection.\nLoop Deogen.                                                     Hide from Deogen.\nStop / crouch carefully around Kormos.                                  Panic sprint near Kormos.\nQuestion every “fast ghost” by condition.                                       Call Raiju/Jinn/Hantu just because footsteps are fast.","status":"baseline"},{"id":15,"title":"Sources & Update Log","page":24,"lastPage":25,"text":"Source hierarchy: official Kinetic Games announcements establish released changes and announced plans. Community mechanics\nsources support the detailed ghost tables. Revision checked October 29, 2026 against Development Preview 22, current official patch\nlistings and event notices. The October 21 PS5 cosmetic hotfix is reported separately because its original Discord notice was not directly\naccessible. New features remain outside the released mechanics tables until launch.\nKinetic Games — Phasmophobia v0.19.0.0: Quality of Life Part 2\nKinetic Games — Quality of Life Part 2 Preview / Restricted map classes\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Evidence\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Guides: Identifying ghosts\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Hunt\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Incense\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Crucifix\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Cursed Possession + individual cursed-item pages\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Equipment + evidence-equipment pages\nPhasmophobia Wiki — Difficulty\nTy Bayn live Phasmophobia cheat sheet (community quick-reference)\nReddit r/PhasmophobiaGame — 2026 zero-evidence / beginner / new-ghost community testing\nSteam Community — current 2026 ghost behavior guide used as a cross-check\nUpdate log\nDate / version                                     What this edition accounts for\nAug 25, 2026 — v0.19.0.0            Restricted Prison/Brownstone/Point Hope; duplicate media indicators; teammate evidence/ghost selections;\n“Items We’ve Found”; custom ghost selection; Oni singing-event sanity fix.\nJul 21–28, 2026 — v0.18.x           Willow rework / QoL Part 1; Deildegast introduced into the current 30-ghost pool and community mechanics\nmatured after release.\n2026 ghost updates               Dayan, Gallu, Obambo, Aswang, Kormos and Deildegast mechanics integrated into tables/zero-evidence decision\nsystem.\nDate / version                                     What this edition accounts for\nThis rebuild                      Converted prior “second-screen cheat sheet” into a dense printable field manual with full dossiers, tier\nmechanics, maps, cursed items, low-evidence systems and community-tested traps.\nSeptember 9, 2026 — lore        Pronunciation and cultural lore for all 30 dossier names; new game-world history and lore section; dated\nexpansion                      source notes and distinction between released content, interpretation and future 1.0 plans.\nSeptember 8 — v0.19.0.1         Prison Entrance gained props and UV interactions. Fixes cover Monkey Paw knowledge exclusions, Ouija\nanswers, camera screens/tripods, UV charging, Restricted-map hides, bone, fuse box and equipment\nplacement. Added VR belt offset and improved grip positions.\nSeptember 16 — v0.19.0.2        Fixed room entry for affected PS5/Xbox players and muffled multiplayer audio after a parabolic\nmicrophone was turned on and dropped.\nOctober 7 — v0.19.1.0          Crimson Eye preparation, Seasonal Event toggle, revive and Easter Egg reward fixes, and two known\nissues. Section 18 gives the current event schedule and complete map list.\nOctober 21 — PS5 report        Reported package 01.000.052 restored the Crimson Eye Twitch Drop tee. Original Discord notice not\ndirectly verified; no global mechanics change inferred. See section 18.\nOctober 29 — editorial revision   Updated event status and charity dates. Added the October 27 Resonance preview as future content,\nretained released mechanics baseline v0.19.1.0, and dated outstanding issues.\nField glossary\nTerm                                                  Meaning in this guide\nFavorite / ghost room              The room selected as the ghost’s anchor. Most ghosts can physically roam away from it.\nGhost location                  Where the ghost entity is actually standing right now; hunts normally start from here.\nBase speed                      Hunt movement speed before ordinary line-of-sight acceleration or a special conditional modifier.\nLOS                                 Line of sight. Most ghosts accelerate while continuously seeing a player; breaking LOS interrupts the chase logic.\nHunt attempt                    The moment the ghost tries to start a hunt. A crucifix can block an attempt before the hunt exists.\nForced evidence                    Evidence guaranteed on 1–2-evidence modes for certain ghosts; disabled on true 0-evidence.\nOfficial hiding spot              A hiding zone controlled by the map/difficulty hiding-place system, not an improvised furniture corner.\nCleansing                         Using incense close enough to the non-hunting ghost to start the hunt-prevention timer and any ghost-specific effects.\nCursed hunt                   A hunt forced by cursed-item mechanics; 1 s grace by default and permanently lengthens later hunts in the contract.\nStrong tell                     A distinctive mechanic worth building an identification around; still verify settings/state when another ghost can imitate it.\nLIVING GUIDE: Phasmophobia is actively updated. When a patch changes ghost evidence, speed, sanity thresholds, equipment, map\nlayouts or bugs, edit the matrix, dossier and quick-reference card together so the print copy cannot contradict itself.","status":"baseline"},{"id":16,"title":"Phasmophobia Lore & Worldbuilding","page":25,"lastPage":27,"text":"Phasmophobia tells most of its story through places, props, recurring imagery and the work of investigating. This section contains\nspoilers for environmental clues and secrets. It separates what the game or developer explicitly presents from interpretations that help\nconnect the clues. Research checked September 9, 2026; the gameplay guide retains its v0.19.1.0 baseline.\nHow to read the evidence for a story\nConfirmed or documented: a detail appears in the game, its text or an official announcement. Community-documented observations are\nuseful but remain distinct from direct developer explanations. Interpretation: a plausible connection between observations that the\ngame has not explicitly settled. Community theory: a proposed explanation, name or chronology that requires further evidence.\nAnnounced future content: an official plan that is not yet part of the released game.\nThese labels matter because a location can contain a fixed corpse, photograph or written clue while its contract ghost is generated\nseparately. Seeing the same model or prop in different places does not by itself prove that a single person is moving between every\nhaunting. An evocative object also need not be a clue to a complete hidden murder plot. [G2]\nThe investigators and the haunted world\nConfirmed premise: the players are paranormal investigators entering haunted locations to gather evidence using specialist equipment.\nThe surrounding work routine—briefing, truck, journal, optional objectives, money and equipment—presents investigation as an\norganized occupation. The tension comes from learning enough to identify the entity while the act of investigating exposes the team to\ndanger. [G1]\nWorldbuilding interpretation: ordinary homes, workplaces and public institutions remain recognizable, making their supernatural\nintrusions disturbing. Equipment turns an invisible presence into incomplete readings: a voice, a trace, a temperature or an image. The\njournal’s categories provide a practical professional vocabulary inside this fictional world. Their authority within a contract should not be\nconfused with an accurate taxonomy of real religions or folklore.\nGameplay and narrative have different jobs. A successful contract ends with evidence and identification; it does not ordinarily include a\nwitnessed exorcism or show a spirit being released. The game does not provide a complete organization chart for the investigators or a\ndocumented explanation for every return visit. Repeat contracts are therefore a weak basis for inventing a continuous biography for a\nresident or ghost. [G1, G2]\nGhost type model and personal identity\nCommunity-documented structure: the contract combines a ghost type with an individual name and other identity properties, including\na physical model. The type chiefly describes rules and behavior. The model is its visible representation. The name identifies the entity for\nthat investigation. These layers can interact, but they are not interchangeable, and models generally do not uniquely identify types. [G2]\nInterpretation: the use of names from many traditions produces a shared horror vocabulary. It does not establish that every story from\nthose traditions is literally true within the game. For example, a Goryo dossier and an Onryo dossier describe different game rules even\nthough the cultural concepts overlap; a Jinn contract does not explain the whole cosmology of jinn belief. The folklore notes beside each\ndossier supply that missing context.\nPatient 07 and the move from Asylum\nDocumented clues: the earlier Asylum contained UV-revealed writing about hoping to leave for Sunny Meadows, together with tally\nmarks. The female patient model carried a Patient 07 label. In Sunny Meadows, a padded room contains many more UV tally marks and\nbloodied handprints, and the morgue includes a corpse associated with that model. These details form the clearest recurring personal\nstory in the older environmental lore. [G3, G10]\nStrong interpretation: the patient hoped a transfer would bring recovery, endured further suffering and died at Sunny Meadows. The\npoignant element is the contrast between the hoped-for refuge and the ruined institution players explore. The environment supports\nmistreatment and a tragic outcome, but it does not supply a complete medical history, named perpetrator or precise cause of death.\n[G10]\nCommunity theory: players often call the character Lisa and turn the tally totals into an exact number of years in confinement. Treat the\nnickname as community terminology and the chronology as conditional: a tally need not represent one day, and the writing is not a\ndated admission record. Stories identifying a particular doctor, diagnosis or ritual cause go beyond the clues listed here. Reddit\ndiscussions and video reconstructions are valuable guides to observations, but their connective narration is not automatically canon.\n[G11]\nSunny Meadows as an institutional horror setting\nDocumented environment: patient wings, a chapel, restricted rooms and a morgue put care, confinement, religion and death in one\nbuilding. Their dereliction suggests a place where treatment failed, without supplying a chronology of its closure. This fictional horror\nsetting is not a description of real psychiatric care. [G3]\nThe chapel’s summoning-circle secret includes inverted or burning crosses. It confirms deliberate occult imagery, not a particular\ncontract ghost or a named cult behind every death. A fixed map secret and the generated ghost type answer different questions. Section\n7 supplies the cursed-possession mechanics. [G3]\nPoint Hope and the drowned\nDocumented clues: Point Hope’s entrance plaque places the lighthouse in Massachusetts and gives 1802 as its construction year. The\nFerryman of the Drowned puzzle culminates in a spectral display around the lighthouse and awards an associated ID card. Together, the\ncoastal setting, the drowned figures and the ferryman wording create a distinct maritime death motif. [G4]\nInterpretation: the lighthouse can be read both as a guide for ships and as a threshold between the living and the dead. “Ferryman”\nevokes the widespread story image of passage into an afterlife. It is a thematic parallel, not confirmation that the Greek Charon\npersonally exists here. The puzzle does not establish a victim list, a particular shipwreck or a single culprit unless further text supplies\nthose details. [G4]\nHomes workplaces and histories left in objects\nOfficial direction: Kinetic described reworked Willow as a place with a story to piece together, continuing its approach to Tanglewood.\nFamiliar rooms can acquire a different emotional history through photographs, everyday objects and interactions. [G8]\nInterpretation should begin with the smallest claim the object supports. A hobby table suggests an interest; it does not alone identify a\nvictim. A damaged room suggests disturbance; it does not name a murderer. Houses, farmhouses, the school, prison, campsites and\ndiner each provide a setting for loss or danger, but atmosphere is not a license to fill missing backstories with invented crimes. Compare\nclues within the same released map version before combining old screenshots with new layouts.\nThe Blood Moon and the Crimson Eye\nOfficial event fiction: the Crimson Eye announcement uses a cult-like invocation directed toward an entity or power associated with a\nblood-red moon. The prose asks for servants, otherworldly access and a promised finality or utopia. The event’s imagery and named\nrewards reinforce that devotional framework. These are deliberate pieces of game fiction rather than fan-created labels. [G5]\nConfirmed gameplay association: Blood Moon conditions make investigations more dangerous and alter rewards. Narrative\ninterpretation: the ritual language suggests people trying to contact or welcome something beyond ordinary hauntings. It leaves major\nquestions unresolved, including the cult’s full membership, the literal identity of the Crimson Eye and how widely its influence extends.\nDo not assume that every summoning circle or red mark elsewhere proves a connection. [G5]\nCursed Hollow and Winter’s Jest\nOfficial event history: Cursed Hollow adds a seasonal ritual framework and supernatural imagery to selected locations. Its event\nobjectives and rewards belong to a particular release and can change on return. These seasonal stories broaden the setting, but the\nexistence of an event does not establish a complete chronology connecting every haunting. [G6]\nWinter’s Jest explicitly brings Krampus into its event fiction. The 2025 event used jack-in-the-box objects and a bag of gifts with a Naughty\nor Nice list, turning familiar holiday imagery into a threatening exchange. This is the game’s adaptation of a traditional seasonal figure;\nits objectives and rewards are game-specific. An event manifestation is not an additional standard ghost type. [G7]\nCursed objects and bargains with consequences\nThe cursed possessions make a coherent narrative pattern: investigators can trade safety for knowledge, contact or control. A mirror\noffers a view, a board offers answers, cards change fortune, and a wish may grant help at a cost. These objects imply that the\nsupernatural can be approached deliberately but is dangerous to bargain with. This is a reading of the mechanics already detailed in\nsection 7, not a new set of gameplay rules.\nLiterary context: W. W. Jacobs’s 1902 story The Monkey’s Paw is a famous example of wishes producing devastating consequences. It is\nuseful background for understanding the item’s horror motif, without asserting an undocumented statement of direct developer intent.\nLikewise, real divination and religious practices have distinct histories; their presentation as interchangeable cursed equipment belongs\nto the game’s fiction. [G12]\nRelease history and the limits of a timeline\nReal release milestones are easier to establish than in-world dates. The game entered Early Access in 2020; Cursed Possessions\nexpanded its dangerous-contact tools in 2021; Apocalypse introduced Sunny Meadows in 2022; Eventide added Point Hope in 2024. Later\nmap reworks and seasonal events expanded the environmental storytelling. These are development dates, not dates when the fictional\nresidents lived or died. [G9]\nThe early 2026 roadmap linked the planned 1.0 release with a horror and lore overhaul. The later Phasmophobia 2026 and Beyond\nannouncement superseded that timetable, moving 1.0 to the second half of 2027. An old preview that says the overhaul arrives in 2026\nshould therefore be read as a former plan. [G13, G8]\nAnnounced future lore and Conduits\nFuture content, not released baseline: Kinetic has announced Conduits for 1.0. These are items tied to particular ghost models, intended\nto replace the collectible Bone. The developer describes a Conduit as an attachment that keeps a ghost connected to the world and\nprevents it from moving on. Revised ghost models, including Patient 07, are also previewed. Their detailed stories and experiences\nremain subject to future release. [G8]\nThis gives an official direction for the worldbuilding: personal objects and particular ghosts may explain more of the haunting. It does\nnot justify retroactively assigning a specific object or life story to every current model. Until that content ships, keep its proposed rules\nout of field identification and avoid treating preview images as completed narrative scenes.\nA practical lore walk\nFor an exploratory session, record the map version, room and exact observation first. Then separate a short interpretation from\nquestions still open. Sunny Meadows offers the patient-room and morgue connection; Point Hope offers the plaque and Ferryman\nimagery; reworked homes invite a closer look at domestic objects. Compare developer text, community screenshots and your own\nobservations before turning a plausible connection into a stated fact.\nUseful unresolved questions include the investigators’ wider organization, the cult’s full aims, the identities behind many ghost models\nand the histories of individual locations. An unanswered question can remain interesting without an invented answer. The strongest lore\nnotes preserve both the clue and the uncertainty so they can be updated when the game reveals more.","status":"baseline"},{"id":17,"title":"Folklore Pronunciation & Sources","page":27,"lastPage":29,"text":"Checked September 9, 2026. Bracketed F references support cultural context; P references support language notes; G references\nsupport game lore. Official game announcements establish developer statements. Academic, archival and dictionary sources establish\nhistorical or linguistic context. Community wikis, videos and discussions are labeled and used with the limitations described in the text.\nRespelling is editorial and approximate, especially for Deildegast, Deogen, Gallu, Kormos and Obambo; it is not a claim of an official\nspoken standard.\nCultural and historical references\n[F1] Aswang Project — The Maximo Ramos Taxonomical Classifications of the Aswang — https://www.aswangproject.com/ramos_aswang/\n[F2] Ask About Ireland — Announcing Death, banshee traditions — https://www.askaboutireland.ie/reading-room/history-heritage/folklore-\nof-ireland/folklore-in-ireland/the-life-cycle/death/announcing-death/\n[F3] Merriam-Webster — The History Behind 8 Halloween Words — https://www.merriam-webster.com/wordplay/the-history-behind-8-\nhalloween-words\n[F4] Wiktionary — Hindi ḍāyan, with Oxford Hindi-English Dictionary reference —\nhttps://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E0%A4%A1%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%A8\n[F5] Store norske leksikon — Deildegast — https://snl.no/deildegast\n[F6] Merriam-Webster — Demon, meanings and word history — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/demon\n[F7] Cryptid Wiki — Deogen; community retelling, not historical verification — https://cryptidz.fandom.com/wiki/Deogen\n[F8] University of Oxford ETCSL — Inana’s descent to the nether world, especially lines 282–305 —\nhttps://etcsl.orinst.ox.ac.uk/section1/tr141.htm\n[F9] Wikipedia — Gallu; secondary orientation to the ancient name — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gallu\n[F10] Kobayashi Norihiko — Spirits of the Dead and Curses or Disasters in Ancient Japan, Kokugakuin Japan Studies — https://k-\nrain.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/2000460/files/KJS_005_001.pdf\n[F11] Wiktionary — Hantu, citing Kamus Dewan and other dictionaries — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hantu\n[F12] Encyclopaedia Iranica — Genie — https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/genie/\n[F13] University of Saskatchewan — Ak Jang in the Context of Altai Religious Tradition, thesis and glossary —\nhttps://harvest.usask.ca/server/api/core/bitstreams/91e8e688-0388-460a-be4e-d443a44872e7/content\n[F14] Wikipedia — Mare in folklore; comparative overview — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mare_%28folklore%29\n[F15] Romulus Antonescu — Dicționar de Simboluri și Credințe Tradiționale Românești, MOROI entry, Romanian heritage database —\nhttps://www.cimec.ro/Etnografie/Antonescu-dictionar/Dictionar-de-Simboluri-Credinte-Traditionale-Romanesti-l-o.html\n[F16] Swedish Institute for Language and Folklore — Mylingen — https://www.isof.se/folkminnen/folkminnesbloggen/inlagg/2024-02-20-\nmylingen\n[F17] Wiktionary — Obake, word formation and meanings — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E3%81%8A%E5%8C%96%E3%81%91\n[F18] Paul Du Chaillu — Stories of the Gorilla Country, historical outsider account; Project Gutenberg —\nhttps://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/52444/pg52444-images.html\n[F19] Wikipedia — Obambo; summary and bibliography of historical accounts — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obambo\n[F20] Smithsonian Libraries — Andreas Marks, Japanese Yōkai and Other Supernatural Beings, collection description —\nhttps://www.si.edu/object/japanese-yokai-and-other-supernatural-beings-authentic-paintings-and-prints-100-ghosts-demons:siris_sil_1167782\n[F21] Nippon.com — Koyama Satoko, Fear and Reverence Japanese Views of Souls Spirits and Ghosts — https://www.nippon.com/en/japan-\ntopics/g02176/\n[F22] Merriam-Webster — Phantom — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/phantom\n[F23] Merriam-Webster — Poltergeist — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/poltergeist\n[F24] Yokai Night Parade — Raijū, with historical-image and museum references — https://yokainightparade.com/en/yokai/raiju/\n[F25] Wikipedia — Revenant; medieval-source overview — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revenant\n[F26] Merriam-Webster — Shade — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/shade\n[F27] Merriam-Webster — Spirit — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/spirit\n[F28] Wikipedia — Thayé; secondary overview of Burmese ghost traditions — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thay%C3%A9\n[F29] Merriam-Webster — Mimic — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/mimic\n[F30] Merriam-Webster — Twin — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/twin\n[F31] Merriam-Webster — Wraith — https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/wraith\nPronunciation references\n[P1] Wiktionary — Aswang, Tagalog and Cebuano pronunciations — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aswang\n[P2] Wiktionary — Dayan pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E0%A4%A1%E0%A4%BE%E0%A4%AF%E0%A4%A8\n[P3] Wiktionary — Goryō pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%BE%A1%E9%9C%8A\n[P4] Wiktionary — Romanian moroi pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moroi\n[P5] Wiktionary — Myling, Swedish word history and dictionary references; reading aid is approximate —\nhttps://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/myling\n[P6] Wiktionary — Obake pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E3%81%8A%E5%8C%96%E3%81%91\n[P7] Wiktionary — Onryō pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E6%80%A8%E9%9C%8A\n[P8] Wiktionary — Raijū pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E9%9B%B7%E7%8D%A3\n[P9] Wiktionary — Burmese thaye pronunciation and ghost meaning — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E1%80%9E%E1%80%9B%E1%80%B2\n[P10] Wiktionary — Yōkai pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A6%96%E6%80%AA\n[P11] Wiktionary — Yūrei pronunciation — https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B9%BD%E9%9C%8A\nGame lore and development references\n[G1] Kinetic Games — Phasmophobia, official game description — https://www.kineticgames.co.uk/phasmophobia\n[G2] Phasmophobia Wiki — Ghost, contract identities and models — https://phasmophobia.fandom.com/wiki/Ghost\n[G3] Phasmophobia Wiki — Sunny Meadows Mental Institution, map clues and Patient 07 —\nhttps://phasmophobia.fandom.com/wiki/Sunny_Meadows_Mental_Institution\n[G4] Phasmophobia Wiki — Point Hope, plaque and Ferryman puzzle — https://phasmophobia.fandom.com/wiki/Point_Hope\n[G5] Kinetic Games — The Crimson Eye Returns, October 2025 — https://kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-crimson-eye-2025\n[G6] Kinetic Games — Announcing Cursed Hollow, April 2025 — https://www.kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-cursed-hollow\n[G7] Kinetic Games — Winter’s Jest Returns, December 2025 — https://kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-winters-jest-returns\n[G8] Kinetic Games — Phasmophobia 2026 and Beyond, revised roadmap and Conduits —\nhttps://www.kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-2026-and-beyond\n[G9] Kinetic Games — News archive, official release history — https://www.kineticgames.co.uk/news\n[G10] Nuzzgard — The Tragic Fate of Patient 07 in Sunny Meadows, community visual investigation — https://www.youtube.com/watch?\nv=RE2vijt88Ws\n[G11] Reddit r/PhasmophobiaGame — Patient 07 discussion with images, December 2024; theories, not canon —\nhttps://www.reddit.com/r/PhasmophobiaGame/comments/1hdlzpx\n[G12] W. W. Jacobs — The Monkey’s Paw, Project Gutenberg, literary source — https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12122\n[G13] Kinetic Games — Phasmophobia’s Roadmap for 2026, earlier plan superseded by G8 —\nhttps://www.kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobias-roadmap-for-2026","status":"baseline"},{"id":18,"title":"October 2026 Update & Crimson Eye","page":30,"lastPage":30,"text":"Revised October 29, 2026. Latest numbered public gameplay patch located: v0.19.1.0. Crimson Eye runs October 8–November 1. The PS5\ncosmetic report is separate. [U1, U2, U12]\nSession settings and fixes\nThe Seasonal Event toggle enables or disables event-map content for a session. The patch smooths the revive white-light transition and\nrepairs an Easter Egg reward unlock failure at a ×0 multiplier. Known issues: Tanglewood exterior lamppost glow on Xbox/Steam;\nunwanted fuse-box visibility on Custom truck maps. [U1]\nThe v0.19.1.0 notes announce no ghost evidence, hunt-threshold, speed, equipment-tier or cursed-possession changes. Keep the\nbaseline tables for released play. The October 27 preview names the next major update Resonance and targets late November; section\n19 separates these plans from live mechanics. [U1, U10]\nCrimson Eye field notes\nEvent locations: 6 Tanglewood Drive, 42 Edgefield Road, Grafton Farmhouse, 13 Willow Street, Bleasdale Farmhouse, standard Point\nHope, Camp Woodwind and Nell’s Diner. Point Hope Restricted is excluded. [U2]\nEarn points through investigations, optional objectives and Blood Moon totems. Blood Moon weather increases danger; keep event\nconditions separate from ordinary identification tests. Rewards include a seasonal tee, Beholder ID Card and badge, Blood Moon charm\nand an upgradeable trophy. Existing trophy holders upgrade theirs. [U2]\nExpired: the tee Twitch Drop ended October 15 at 11:59 PM BST; double XP/rewards ended October 22. Crimson Eye ends November 1.\nThe later charity notice dates Ghost Hunts for Hearts October 26–November 2. Participating fundraising streams offer the Advocate\ncard/badge; an animated set depends on a fundraising goal. Check campaign availability. [U2, U11]\nCorrections and source status\nRetained corrections: Kormos through-wall killing was fixed in July; Aswang can revisit its remembered hide during grace; Dayan\nproximity spans floors; Obambo switches after one minute, then every two minutes. The latter mechanics are community-documented.\n[U4, U6–U9]\nSee section 15 for September fixes. Earlier known issues are dated reports; persistence is not guaranteed. Keep new behavior reports\nprovisional pending reproduction or confirmation.\nOctober 21 PS5 hotfix report\nReported October 21 PS5 package 01.000.052 restored the Crimson Eye Twitch Drop tee, according to a community relay of Discord.\nOriginal notice not directly verified; no PC/Xbox, crash, audio or ghost-mechanics fix established. [U12]\nUpdate sources\n[U1] Kinetic Games v0.19.1.0\nhttps://kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-v01910-patch-notes\n[U2] Kinetic Games Crimson Eye 2026\nhttps://www.kineticgames.co.uk/news/the-crimson-eye-approaches-once-more\n[U3] Kinetic Games v0.19.0.1\nhttps://kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-v0-19-0-1-patch-notes\n[U4] Kinetic Games v0.18.0.0\nhttps://kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-v0-18-0-0-patch-notes\n[U5] Kinetic Games v0.19.0.2\nhttps://kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-v01902-patch-notes\n[U6] Phasmophobia Wiki Aswang — community mechanics\nhttps://phasmophobia.fandom.com/wiki/Aswang\n[U7] Phasmophobia Wiki Dayan — community mechanics\nhttps://phasmophobia.fandom.com/wiki/Dayan\n[U8] Phasmophobia Wiki Kormos — community mechanics\nhttps://phasmophobia.fandom.com/wiki/Kormos\n[U9] Phasmophobia Wiki Obambo — community mechanics\nhttps://phasmophobia.fandom.com/wiki/Obambo\n[U10] Kinetic Games Development Preview 22, October 27, 2026\nhttps://kineticgames.co.uk/news/phasmophobia-development-preview-22\n[U11] Kinetic Games Ghost Hunts for Hearts Returns, October 13, 2026\nhttps://www.kineticgames.co.uk/news/ghost-hunts-for-hearts-returns\n[U12] Reddit PS5 update report, October 21, 2026; secondary relay of Discord notice\nhttps://www.reddit.com/r/PhasmophobiaGame/comments/1xbugla/ps5_update_today/","status":"seasonal"},{"id":19,"title":"Resonance Preview & Release Checklist","page":31,"lastPage":31,"text":"ANNOUNCED CONTENT • NOT RELEASED AS OF OCTOBER 29, 2026\nKinetic’s October 27 preview combines Unity 6 and the Content Update as Resonance, targeted for late November. An exact release date\nand final patch notes are still pending. [U10]\nWhat is planned\nDowsing Rods locate the Bone: Tier 1 gives a broad direction; Tier 3 improves precision and floor guidance.\nSound Sensor map icons distinguish sound types on the player’s floor and support movement-speed observation.\nNew ghost-interaction voice commands also support Text mode; VR text users access commands through the watch. A Journal page lists\nphrases.\nRandom-map selection gains custom pools and map-size filters.\nUnity 6 targets stability, memory and performance, with lighting, graphics presets, VR Eye Adaptation and colour-vision options.\nPlayer conduct warnings and report-outcome feedback are planned alongside Xbox Play Anywhere.\nThe Player Character Visual Update is delayed beyond Resonance. Edgefield’s rework remains planned for later 2026.\nThese are preview commitments, not tested live rules. Do not add Dowsing Rods to the current equipment list or use the new Sound\nSensor interface as an available identification test yet. [U10]\nAfter Resonance launches\nRead the release notes before changing the edition label. Confirm each equipment tier’s unlock, cost, range, limitations and controls.\nVerify whether any ghost numbers or evidence changed; update the matrix, dossier, speed index and quick cards together. Recheck\nevent modifiers separately. Test map and media changes, then export and inspect a new PDF.\nUntil then, use sections 1–17 for the existing field rules and section 18 for current event and maintenance context. Preview artwork and\ndemonstrations are not substitutes for final mechanics documentation.","status":"preview"}]};
